st":"Ruben Julius","key":"eckpau","last":"Eck","name":"Eck, Ruben Julius","rank":55,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40023811"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/aas.70009","issn":["00015172"],"issue":"4","journal":"Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica","paperid":"e70009","publisher":"Blackwell Munksgaard","status":"p","title":"Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica","volume":"69"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-86000200215"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Other Hospitals","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","copenhagen university hospital - holbæk"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-49298cf5-d3a4-41df-9a3d-e047bf7f31ae","ku-c07f3e0e-d741-40d5-adcc-c1ca0f9616fd","sdu-9ed77811-26c4-4d31-989a-97ab5a7e6bc5"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Preferences for thromboprophylaxis in the intensive care unit : An international survey","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-085.54-803674","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"The initial evaluation of stenosis during coronary angiography is typically performed by visual assessment. Visual assessment has limited accuracy compared to fractional flow reserve and quantitative coronary angiography, which are more time-consuming and costly. Applying deep learning might yield a faster and more accurate stenosis assessment. We developed a deep learning model to classify cine loops into left or right coronary artery (LCA/RCA) or \"other\". Data were obtained by manual annotation. Using these classifications, cine loops before revascularization were identified and curated automatically. Separate deep learning models for LCA and RCA were developed to estimate stenosis using these identified cine loops. From a cohort of 19,414 patients and 332,582 cine loops, we identified cine loops for 13,480 patients for model development and 5056 for internal testing. External testing was conducted using automated identified cine loops from 608 patients. For identification of significant stenosis (visual assessment of diameter stenosis > 70%), our model obtained a receiver operator characteristic (ROC) area under the curve (ROC-AUC) of 0.903 (95% CI: 0.900-0.906) on the internal test. The performance was evaluated on the external test set against visual assessment, 3D quantitative coronary angiography, and fractional flow reserve (≤ 0.80), obtaining ROC AUC values of 0.833 (95% CI: 0.814-0.852), 0.798 (95% CI: 0.741-0.842), and 0.780 (95% CI: 0.743-0.817), respectively. The deep-learning-based stenosis estimation models showed promising results for predicting stenosis. Compared to previous work, our approach demonstrates performance increase, includes all 16 segments, does not exclude revascularized patients, is externally tested, and is simpler using fewer steps.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Eschen, Christian Kim","Banasik, Karina","Dahl, Anders Bjorholm","Chmura, Piotr Jaroslaw","Bruun-Rasmussen, Peter","Pedersen, Frants","Køber, Lars","Engstrøm, Thomas","Bøttcher, Morten","Winther, Simon","Christensen, Alex Hørby","Bundgaard, Henning","Brunak, Søren"],"cuid":["S03-087.87-813677"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"keyword":["Coronary Angiography","Coronary artery disease","Deep learning","Ischemic heart disease","Myocardial Infarction","Quantitative coronary angiography"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"automated stenosis estimation of coronary angiographies using end to end learning","src":["au","dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10554-025-03324-x","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-087.87-813677","identifier":[{"id":"85217418021","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11880145","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39789341","type":"pmid"},{"id":"001394658200001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-6d1fcb13-bd0b-4cbb-b87c-5b004ac0fe7f","dtu-c9a6ff83-aa5f-4574-8210-fc20993904f2","ku-c484486d-49ee-4f2c-bd0a-8b24269fcb97"],"keyword":["Coronary angiography","Coronary artery disease","Deep learning","Ischemic heart disease","Myocardial infarction","Quantitative coronary angiography"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-E22A649C2499A5F633A158E48F1D4AC7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10554-025-03324-x","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/397666988/s10554-025-03324-x.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"acces