Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-da7788cb-ad09-43c5-92cc-f8004fcdad78","ku-bc94d70e-251a-4552-a272-bbeb27344ffa"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Microbiological and decomposition analysis of mass mink burial sites during the COVID-19 pandemic","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-761918","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Pituitary adenylate cyclase-activating peptide (PACAP) is a neuropeptide pivotal in migraine pathophysiology and is considered a promising new migraine drug target. Although intravenous PACAP triggers migraine attacks and a recent phase II trial with a PACAP-inhibiting antibody showed efficacy in migraine prevention, targeting the PACAP receptor PAC1 alone has been unsuccessful. The present study investigated the role of three PACAP receptors (PAC1, VPAC1 and VPAC2) in inducing migraine-relevant hypersensitivity in mice. METHODS: Hindpaw hypersensitivity was induced by repeated PACAP38 injections. Tactile sensitivity responses were quantified using von Frey filaments in three knockout (KO) mouse strains, each lacking one of the PACAP-receptors (Ntotal = 160). Additionally, ex vivo wire myography was used to assess vasoactivity of the carotid artery, and gene expression of PACAP receptors was examined by qPCR. RESULTS: PACAP38 induced hypersensitivity in WT controls (p < 0.01) that was diminished in VPAC1 and VPAC2 KO mice (p < 0.05). In contrast, PAC1 KO mice showed similar responses to WT controls (p > 0.05). Myograph experiments supported these findings showing diminished vasoactivity in VPAC1 and VPAC2 KO mice. We found no upregulation of the non-modified PACAP receptors in KO mice. CONCLUSIONS: This study assessed all three PACAP receptors in a migraine mouse model and suggests a significant role of VPAC receptors in migraine pathophysiology. The lack of hypersensitivity reduction in PAC1 KO mice suggests the involvement of other PACAP receptors or compensatory mechanisms. The results indicate that targeting only individual PACAP receptors may not be an effective migraine treatment.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Guo, Song","Rasmussen, Rikke Holm","Hay-Schmidt, Anders","Ashina, Messoud","Asuni, Ayodeji A.","Jensen, Jeppe Møller","Holm, Anja","Lauritzen, Sabrina Prehn","Dorsam, Glenn","Hannibal, Jens","Georg, Birgitte","Kristensen, David Møbjerg","Olesen, Jes","Christensen, Sarah Louise"],"cuid":["S03-093.62-755852"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Christensen, Sarah Louise"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center","Harvard Medical School","Harvard University","North Dakota State University"],"keyword":["Animals","Disease Models Animal","Hyperalgesia physiopathology","Knockout mice","Male","Mice","Mice Inbred C57BL","Mice Knockout","Migraine","Migraine Disorders chemically induced","PAC1","PACAP","Receptors Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide Type II metabolism","Receptors Vasoactive Intestinal Polypeptide Type I metabolism","VPAC1","VPAC2","Vasodilation drug effects","von Frey"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-LUND"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-LUND"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-NP-LUND","DI-PR-LUND","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"vpac000000000001 and vpac000000000002 receptors mediate tactile hindpaw hypersensitivity and carotid artery dilatation induced by pacap000000000038 in a migraine relevant mouse model","src":["aau","ku","ruc"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-RUC"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s10194-024-01830-2","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Open access funding provided by Copenhagen University. Song Guo is supported by the BRIDGE \\u2013 Translational Excellence Programme (bridge.ku.dk) at the Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, University of Copenhagen, funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation. Grant agreement no. NNF20SA0064340 (2021 fellows). Also, this work was supported by Candys Foundation. Messoud Ashina was supported by the Lundbeck Foundation Professor Grant (R310-2018\\u20133711). JH in supported from the Danish Biotechnology Center for Cellular Communication."],"id":"S03-093.62-755852","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11293201","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85200292010","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39085771","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-8288436c-1bbf-4acf-95f2-21d32da532ab","ku-39086323-fab8-4348-9eec-f1d76e97e89b","ruc-8684a28c-0d41-489d-9885-9a340ef446aa"],"keyword":["Animals","Carotid Arteries/drug effects","Disease Models, Animal","Hindlimb/physiopathology","Hyperalgesia/physiopathology","Knockout mice","Male","Mice","Mice, Inbred C57BL","Mice, Knockout","Migraine","Migraine Disorders/chemically induced","PAC1","PACAP","Pituitary Adenylate Cyclase-Activating Polypeptide/pharmacology","Receptors, Pituitary Adenylate Cyclase-Activating Polypeptide, Type I/metabolism","Receptors, Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide, Type II/metabolism","Receptors, Vasoactive Intestinal Polypeptide, Type I/metabolism","VPAC1","VPAC2","Vasodilation/drug effects","von Frey"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-D406DF60B322A94DDE927C79AC0AE575"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/740092885/Guo_et_al._2024_._VPAC1_and_VPAC2_receptors_mediate_tactile_hindpaw_hypersensitivity_and_carotid_artery_dilatation_induced_by_PACAP38_in_a_migraine_relevant_mouse_model.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://rucforsk.ruc.dk/ws/files/105783072/s10194-024-01830-2.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s10194-024-01830-2","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/40161660