{"abstract":"Extraintestinal pathogenic Escherichia coli (ExPEC) can lead to severe infections, with additional risks of increasing antimicrobial resistance rates. Genotypic similarities between ExPEC and avian pathogenic E. coli (APEC) support a possible role for a poultry meat reservoir in human disease. Some genomic studies have been done on the ST117 lineage which contaminates poultry meat, carries multidrug resistance, can be found in the human intestinal microbiota, and causes human extraintestinal disease. This study analyzed the genomes of 61 E. coli from Brazilian poultry outbreaks focusing on ST117, to further define its possible zoonotic characteristics by genotypic and phylogenomic analyses, along with 1,699 worldwide ST117 isolates originating from human, animal, and environment sources. A predominance of ST117 was detected in the Brazilian isolates (n = 20/61) frequently carrying resistance to critical antibiotics (>86%) linked to IncFII, IncI1, or IncX4 replicons. High similarities were found between IncX4 from Brazilian outbreaks and those from E. coli recovered from imported Brazilian poultry meat and human clinical cases. The ST117 phylogeny showed non-specificity according to host and continent and an AMR index score indicated the highest resistance in Asia and South America, with the latter statistically more resistant and overrepresented with resistance to extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBL). Most ST117 human isolates were predicted to have a poultry origin (93%, 138/148). In conclusion, poultry is a likely source for zoonotic ExPEC strains, particularly the ST117 lineage which can also serve as a reservoir for resistance determinants against critical antibiotics encoded on highly transmissible plasmids. IMPORTANCE: Certain extraintestinal pathogenic Escherichia coli (ExPEC) are particularly important as they affect humans and animals. Lineages, such as ST117, are predominant in poultry and frequent carriers of antibiotic resistance, presenting a risk to humans handling or ingesting poultry products. We analyzed ExPEC isolates causing outbreaks in Brazilian poultry, focusing on the ST117 as the most detected lineage. Genomic comparisons with international isolates from humans and animals were performed describing the potential zoonotic profile. The Brazilian ST117 isolates carried resistance determinants against critical antibiotics, mainly on plasmids, in some cases identical to those carried by international isolates. South American ST117 isolates from all sources generally exhibit more resistance, including to critical antibiotics, and worldwide, the vast majority of human isolates belonging to this lineage have a predicted poultry origin. As the world's largest poultry exporter, Brazil has an important role in developing strategies to prevent the dissemination of multidrug-resistant zoonotic ExPEC strains.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Saidenberg, Andre Becker Simoes","Edslev, S. M.","Hallstrøm, S.","Rasmussen, A.","Park, D. E.","Aziz, M.","dos Santos Queiroz, B.","Baptista, A. A. S.","Barbosa, F.","Rocha, V. G. P.","van Vliet, Arnoud H. M.","Dalsgaard, Anders","Price, L. B.","Knöbl, T.","Stegger, M."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-764679"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","America, South","Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Brazil","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","George Washington University","Murdoch University","Universidade Estadual de Londrina","Universidade de São Paulo","University of Surrey"],"keyword":["Escherichia coli","ExPEC","Poultry","ST117"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"i escherichia coli i st000000000117 exploring the zoonotic hypothesis","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.00466-24","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-764679","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11448156","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85205741294","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39235965","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-1a3b9f30-c56b-440e-99bd-f3249c9d52a0"],"keyword":["Escherichia coli","ExPEC","FZEC","ST117","poultry"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2C194B41A1DEDB616C14F10458503F52"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.00466-24","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/409349963/Escherichia_coli_ST117.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Food Safety and Zoonoses"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ssi statens serum institut","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-SSI","l3nn":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","namevar":["Statens Serum Institut"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-SSI","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"br","key":"universidade de são paulo","loc":["Brazil","America, South"],"name":"Universidade de São Paulo","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"george washington university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"George Washington University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"br","key":"universidade estadual de londrina","loc":["Brazil","America, South"],"name":"Universidade Estadual de Londrina","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"gb","key":"university of surrey","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Surrey","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"au","key":"murdoch university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Murdoch University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"A. B. S.","key":"saidenbergpau","last":"Saidenberg","locid":"242a7d5b-1818-40a9-81b8-65fa4d44c7e7","name":"Saidenberg, Andre Becker Simoes","name_org":"Saidenberg, A. B. S.","orcid":"0000-0002-2672-0749","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"S. M.","key":"edslevpau","last":"Edslev","name":"Edslev, S. M.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"S.","key":"hallstrømpau","last":"Hallstrøm","name":"Hallstrøm, S.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"A.","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, A.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"D. E.","key":"parkpau","last":"Park","name":"Park, D. E.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"M.","key":"azizpau","last":"Aziz","name":"Aziz, M.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"B.","key":"dossantosqueirozpau","last":"dos Santos Queiroz","name":"dos Santos Queiroz, B.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"A. A. S.","key":"baptistapau","last":"Baptista","name":"Baptista, A. A. S.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"F.","key":"barbosapau","last":"Barbosa","name":"Barbosa, F.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"V. G. P.","key":"rochapau","last":"Rocha","name":"Rocha, V. G. P.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","first":"Arnoud H. M.","key":"vanvlietpau","last":"van Vliet","name":"van Vliet, Arnoud H. M.","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"A.","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"ffab842e-9b80-4860-b92f-967f5009c779","name":"Dalsgaard, Anders","name_org":"Dalsgaard, A.","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"L. B.","key":"pricepau","last":"Price","name":"Price, L. B.","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"T.","key":"knöblpau","last":"Knöbl","name":"Knöbl, T.","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4 7","first":"M.","key":"steggerpau","last":"Stegger","name":"Stegger, M.","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39235965"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1128/spectrum.00466-24","issn":["21650497"],"issue":"10","journal":"Microbiology Spectrum","paperid":"e00466-24","status":"p","title":"Microbiology Spectrum","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85205741294"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-1a3b9f30-c56b-440e-99bd-f3249c9d52a0"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"<i>Escherichia coli </i>ST117 : exploring the zoonotic hypothesis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-764679","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"African antelope diversity is a globally unique vestige of a much richer world-wide Pleistocene megafauna. Despite this, the evolutionary processes leading to the prolific radiation of African antelopes are not well understood. Here, we sequenced 145 whole genomes from both subspecies of the waterbuck (Kobus ellipsiprymnus), an African antelope believed to be in the process of speciation. We investigated genetic structure and population divergence and found evidence of a mid-Pleistocene separation on either side of the eastern Great Rift Valley, consistent with vicariance caused by a rain shadow along the so-called “Kingdon’s Line.” However, we also found pervasive evidence of both recent and widespread historical gene flow across the Rift Valley barrier. By inferring the genome-wide landscape of variation among subspecies, we found 14 genomic regions of elevated differentiation, including a locus that may be related to each subspecies’ distinctive coat pigmentation pattern. We investigated these regions as candidate speciation islands. However, we observed no significant reduction in gene flow in these regions, nor any indications of selection against hybrids. Altogether, these results suggest a pattern whereby climatically driven vicariance is the most important process driving the African antelope radiation and suggest that reproductive isolation may not set in until very late in the divergence process. This has a significant impact on taxonomic inference, as many taxa will be in a gray area of ambiguous systematic status, possibly explaining why it has been hard to achieve consensus regarding the species status of many African antelopes. Our analyses demonstrate how population genetics based on low-depth whole genome sequencing can provide new insights that can help resolve how far lineages have gone along the path to speciation.","author_count":23,"contributors":["Wang, Xi","Pedersen, Casper-Emil Tingskov","Athanasiadis, Georgios","Garcia-Erill, Genís","Hanghøj, Kristian","Bertola, Laura D.","Rasmussen, Malthe Sebro","Schubert, Mikkel","Liu, Xiaodong","Li, Zilong","Lin, Long","Balboa, Renzo F.","Jørsboe, Emil","Nursyifa, Casia","Liu, Shanlin","Muwanika, Vincent","Masembe, Charles","Chen, Lei","Wang, Wen","Moltke, Ida","Siegismund, Hans Redlef","Albrechtsen, Anders","Heller, Rasmus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776880"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Africa","Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["China","Spain","Uganda","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","China Agricultural University","Makerere University","Northwestern Polytechnical University Xian","University of Barcelona","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Admixture","Waterbuck"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"persistent gene flow suggests an absence of reproductive isolation in an african antelope speciation model","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/sysbio/syae037","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776880","identifier":[{"id":"85205914343","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC11637686","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39140829","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-08476761-02d1-4f73-a2ee-94675a0ab77b"],"keyword":["Admixture","islands of differentiation","speciation genomics","waterbuck"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C19C3032E312E4BBABCA895FFF963E4B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/413568077/syae037.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC"