affno":"5","first":"Bruce H.","key":"vaughnpau","last":"Vaughn","name":"Vaughn, Bruce H.","rank":18,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bo M.","key":"vintherpau","last":"Vinther","locid":"0179bd8e-644a-4f98-a641-1bc374aabdd8","name":"Vinther, Bo Møllesøe","name_org":"Vinther, Bo M.","orcid":"0000-0002-6078-771X","rank":19,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"James W.c.","key":"whitepau","last":"White","name":"White, James W.c.","rank":20,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39432793"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1073/pnas.2402637121","issn":["00278424","10916490"],"issn_org":["00278424"],"issue":"44","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","paperid":"e2402637121","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","volume":"121"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85206961867"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-5b82b9e0-ec80-4412-8ea3-43376034b674"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Greenland spatial fingerprint of Dansgaard–Oeschger events in observations and models","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-777810","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: The study of the effectiveness of multidisciplinary rehabilitation (MDR) and how the symptoms and needs of individuals with multiple sclerosis (MS) interplay with the diversity of MDR interventions is still a conundrum, often referred to as a black box. METHODS: We conducted a partial crossover randomized controlled trial with follow-ups at 1 (discharge), 6, and 12 months. Based on their rehabilitation goals, each patient was categorized into 1 of 5 main focus areas (MFAs) prior to admission: Resilience, Cognitive Function, Energy, Physical Function, and Personal Needs. The Functional Assessment of Multiple Sclerosis (FAMS) instrument scores were the primary outcome. RESULTS: MFA groups varied in age (P =.036), MS type (P =.002), Expanded Disability Status Scale score (P <.001), time since diagnosis (P =.002), and FAMS at baseline (P <.001), as well as in composition and quantity of MDR services. At discharge, significant FAMS improvements were found in all 5 MFA groups (FAMS change?>10.4, P <.05), but the affected subdimensions and persistence of improvements varied among MFA groups. At the 6-month follow-up, estimates of controlled differences in FAMS were 9.9 (P =.001), 5.6 (P =.196), 8.5 (P =.008), –1.4 (P =.548), and 17.9 (P =.012) for the Resilience, Cognitive Function, Energy, Physical Function, and Personal Needs groups, respectively. CONCLUSIONS: This study demonstrated that inpatient MDR improves functioning and health-related quality of life in people with MS; the type, degree, and persistence of the benefits are associated with a patient’s main focus area of rehabilitation, which signifies the importance of the goal-setting process in MDR.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Trénel, Philipp","Boesen, Finn","Skjerbæk, Anders Guldhammer","Petersen, Thor","Rasmussen, Peter Vestergaard","Nørgaard, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-783616"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_si