2.0-85181235598"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Other Hospitals","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Undetermined","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","collaboration for research in intensive care"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-9410a21e-4879-4681-a535-8c311fcff681","au-095ba0bc-4cd6-48dd-a49c-04cc1506a342","ku-cb4ab328-91d9-4adb-86a0-9e0f075c250f","sdu-f323383b-180a-45b6-a420-f443676d45ed"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Long-term outcomes with haloperidol versus placebo in acutely admitted adult ICU patients with delirium","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S04-095.92-724259","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Several African mammals exhibit a phylogeographic pattern where closely related taxa are split between West/Central and East/Southern Africa, but their evolutionary relationships and histories remain controversial. Bushpigs (Potamochoerus larvatus) and red river hogs (P. porcus) are recognised as separate species due to morphological distinctions, a perceived lack of interbreeding at contact, and putatively old divergence times, but historically, they were considered conspecific. Moreover, the presence of Malagasy bushpigs as the sole large terrestrial mammal shared with the African mainland raises intriguing questions about its origin and arrival in Madagascar. Analyses of 67 whole genomes revealed a genetic continuum between the two species, with putative signatures of historical gene flow, variable F ST values, and a recent divergence time (<500,000 years). Thus, our study challenges key arguments for splitting Potamochoerus into two species and suggests their speciation might be incomplete. Our findings also indicate that Malagasy bushpigs diverged from southern African populations and underwent a limited bottleneck 1000-5000 years ago, concurrent with human arrival in Madagascar. These results shed light on the evolutionary history of an iconic and widespread African mammal and provide insight into the longstanding biogeographic puzzle surrounding the bushpig’s presence in Madagascar.","author_count":34,"contributors":["Balboa, Renzo F.","Bertola, Laura D.","Brüniche-Olsen, Anna","Rasmussen, Malthe Sebro","Liu, Xiaodong","Besnard, Guillaume","Salmona, Jordi","Santander, Cindy G.","He, Shixu","Zinner, Dietmar","Pedrono, Miguel","Muwanika, Vincent","Masembe, Charles","Schubert, Mikkel","Kuja, Josiah","Quinn, Liam","Garcia-Erill, Genís","Stæger, Frederik Filip","Rakotoarivony, Rianja","Henrique, Margarida","Lin, Long","Wang, Xi","Heaton, Michael P.","Smith, Timothy P.L.","Hanghøj, Kristian","Sinding, Mikkel Holger S.","Atickem, Anagaw","Chikhi, Lounès","Roos, Christian","Gaubert, Philippe","Siegismund, Hans Redlef","Moltke, Ida","Albrechtsen, Anders","Heller, Rasmus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-719191"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Africa","America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Ethiopia","France","Germany","Portugal","Uganda","United States"],"glorg":["Addis Ababa University","All Global Affiliations","German Primate Center – Leibniz Institute for Primate Research","Instituto Gulbenkian de Ciência","Makerere University","The French Agricultural Research Centre for International Development (CIRAD)","United States Department of Agriculture","University of Göttingen","University of Porto","Université Paul Sabatier Toulouse III"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"african bushpigs exhibit porous species boundaries and appeared in madagascar concurrently with human arrival","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-44105-1","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-719191","identifier":[{"id":"85181229754","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10764920","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"38172616","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-079fec98-1d2a-4805-934a-b7ccc61878c1"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6E040EA8817DC1C4ACA5B5E4883D02C0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/380206106/s41467_023_44105_1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-44105-1","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2