{"abstract":"Psychopharmacological treatment may be an independent risk factor for increased length of stay and readmission after hip and knee replacement. Thus, temporary perioperative discontinuation may be beneficial. However, little is known regarding the treatments, and not all are feasible to discontinue. Therefore, the aim of this study was to describe the treatments in terms of type, dose, duration, indication and initiating physician to assess the feasibility of temporary perioperative discontinuation. We included 482 patients planned for hip or knee replacement in psychopharmacological treatment for psychiatric disorders from 2021 to 2023 at five orthopaedic departments in Denmark. Most patients were treated with antidepressants (89%); most frequently, either selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs; 48%) or serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs; 21%). The majority received monotherapy (70%); most frequently, an SSRI (36%) or an SNRI (12%). Most antidepressants were initiated by general practitioners (71%), and the treatments had lasted for more than a year (87%). The doses of SSRIs/SNRIs were moderate, and the most frequent indication for antidepressants was depression (77%). These results imply that temporary perioperative SSRI/SNRI discontinuation may be feasible in hip and knee replacement patients and support a future randomized controlled trial investigating the potential benefits of temporary discontinuation.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Kornvig, Simon","Kehlet, Henrik","Jørgensen, Christoffer Calov","Fink-Jensen, Anders","Videbech, Poul","Lindberg-Larsen, Martin","Gromov, Kirill","Rasmussen, Mathias Bæk","Bieder, Manuel Josef","Varnum, Claus"],"cuid":["S02-095.92-741493"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Kornvig, Simon"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Aged 80 and over","Antidepressants","Antidepressive Agents therapeutic use","Arthroplasty Replacement Hip","Arthroplasty Replacement Knee","Denmark","Depression drug therapy","Discontinuation","Female","Hip","Humans","Knee","Length of Stay statistics numerical data","Male","Mental Disorders drug therapy","Middle Aged","Replacement"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-LUND"],"OT":["Centre for Fast-track Hip and Knee Replacement"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-NP-LUND","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"psychopharmacological treatment in patients planned for hip or knee replacement","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/bcpt.14017","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The present study is a part of the fast\\u2010track project by the Centre for Fast\\u2010track Hip and Knee Replacement, which has received funding from the NOVO Nordisk Foundation (grant number: NNF21SA0073760) in 2021. SK has received funding for its salary from the University of Southern Denmark and the Region of Southern Denmark."],"id":"S02-095.92-741493","identifier":[{"id":"85192818849","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38726877","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-a9b3ac21-ebce-4240-91ad-dc65a4a04b39","sdu-0757e5aa-ea75-4d0b-8c2e-590f05f70f27"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Aged, 80 and over","Antidepressive Agents/therapeutic use","Arthroplasty, Replacement, Hip","Arthroplasty, Replacement, Knee","Denmark","Depression/drug therapy","Female","Humans","Length of Stay/statistics & numerical data","Male","Mental Disorders/drug therapy","Middle Aged","Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors/adm