{"abstract":"Three trace elements in human bones permit the delineation of temporal and social variability among medieval to early modern Danes in what they ate (strontium, Sr) and whether they lived in an urban or non-urban setting (lead, Pb; copper, Cu). The chemical composition of bones from 332 children (5 to 12 years old) buried in 51 Danish cemeteries was estimated through Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass Spectrometry (ICP-MS). Children provide a local chemical signal because they were less likely than adults to have moved from one place to another. There was no age effect on trace element concentrations. Geographical variability in trace element concentrations was highly localized, so the three elements, individually or collectively, cannot be used to identify where in Denmark people originated. Diets and exposure to sources of Pb and Cu, however, did not remain constant over time. Trace element concentrations show that the life experiences of people from towns differed from their rural counterparts. While most apparent with Pb and Cu, it is also true of Sr until urban and rural diets converged in the early modern period.Keywords: Historic Denmark; Bioavailable trace elements; Social variation; Temporal variation; Human skeletons; ICP-MS","author_count":39,"contributors":["Boldsen, Jesper Lier","Dangvard Pedersen, Dorthe","Milner, George R.","Kristensen, Vicki Rytoft Lillegaard","Skytte, Lilian","Møller, Stig Bergmann","Sarauw, Torben Birk","Andersen, Charlotte Boje Hilligsø","Larsen, Lars Agersnap","Hyldgaard, Inger Marie","Klingenberg, Mette","Larsen, Lars Krants","Mollerup, Lene","Seberg, Lone","Bentsen, Lars Christian","Søvsø, Morten","Kristensen, Tenna","Christensen, Jakob Tue","Heide, Poul Baltzer","Nørgaard, Lone C.","Uldum, Otto","Engberg, Niels","Simonsen, Rikke","Dahlstrøm, Hanna","Langkilde, Jesper","Wickman, Niels","Hansen, Palle Birk","Wille-Jørgensen, Dorthe","Stjernqvist, Kasper Wurr","Rasmussen, Anders","Rasmussen, Kaare Lund"],"cuid":["S02-096.40-735605"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Boldsen, Jesper Lier"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Department of Anthropology, Pennsylvania State University, USA","The Pennsylvania State University"],"keyword":["Human Skeletons","ICP MS","Social variation","Temporal variation"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-KUM"],"OT":["Roskilde Museum"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-KUM","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"variation in bioavailable lead copper and strontium concentrations in human skeletons from medieval to early modern denmark","src":["kum","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-GV-KUM","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaa.2024.101587","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Many institutions granted access to the skeletal remains examined here, as detailed in Table 1 and the Supplemental Information . This research was supported by a grant from DFF – FKK: 6107-00284B."],"id":"S02-096.40-735605","identifier":[{"id":"85186598072","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["kum-4f82d25d-066c-4fde-8f89-352051280904","sdu-8c0a8a75-ac1d-435d-8abe-87424821d21d"],"keyword":["Bioavailable trace elements","Historic Denmark","Human skeletons","ICP-MS","Social variation","Temporal variation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-E566CF4C6A355558584D6EA848FFCF96"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaa.2024.101587","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearc