endorthepau","last":"Wille-Jørgensen","name":"Wille-Jørgensen, Dorthe","rank":28,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kasper","key":"wurrstjernqvistkasperpau","last":"Wurr Stjernqvist","locid":"74ddb89a-d000-442a-b6d4-d97e604e98d5","name":"Wurr Stjernqvist, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0001-6231-2331","rank":29,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"rasmussenanderspau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Anders","rank":30,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kaare Lund","key":"rasmussenkaarelundpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"1d3bf45c-d029-45f5-a60e-06f0c3d04592","name":"Rasmussen, Kaare Lund","orcid":"0000-0001-9575-868X","rank":31,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jaa.2024.101587","issn":["02784165","10902686"],"issn_org":["02784165"],"journal":"Journal of Anthropological Archaeology","paperid":"101587","publisher":"Academic Press Inc.","status":"p","title":"Journal of Anthropological Archaeology","volume":"74"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85186598072"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["kum-4f82d25d-066c-4fde-8f89-352051280904","sdu-8c0a8a75-ac1d-435d-8abe-87424821d21d"],"src":["KUM Ministry of Culture - Archives, Museums, and Royal Library Denmark","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Variation in bioavailable lead, copper, and strontium concentrations in human skeletons from medieval to early modern Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.40-735605","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Objective Poor cardiorespiratory fitness and health is common among childcare workers. We designed the 'Goldilocks-games' according to the Goldilocks Work principle to provide high-intensity physical activity for childcare workers. We investigated the effectiveness of this Goldilocks Work intervention in increasing occupational high-intensity physical activity and improving work-related health. Methods In a two-arm cluster randomized trial, 16 childcare institutions with 142 workers were randomly allocated to either an 8-week Goldilocks Work intervention or a control group. The primary outcome was occupational time in high-intensity physical activity. Secondary outcomes were occupational time in active physical behaviors, heart rate during sleep, pain, physical exhaustion, energy at work, work productivity, and need for recovery. Results The intervention was successfully delivered and received. Both groups had a low amount of occupational high-intensity physical activity at baseline, and the intervention group reported playing the games 3.1 [standard deviation (SD) 1.5] times/week for a duration of 112.2 (SD 175.0) min/week. However, the intervention did not increase high-intensity physical activity or the secondary outcomes, except for energy at work, measured on a scale from 0–10, increasing 0.65 [95% confidence interval (CI) 0.08–1.21], and need for recovery, measured on a scale from 1–5, decreasing-0.32 (95% CI,-0.54–-0.09). Conclusion The intervention was successfully delivered and received, but did not increase high-intensity physical activity. The intervention group increased their energy at work and decreased their need for recovery, but not the other health-related outcomes. Further research on how to design and implement health-promoting work environment interventions in childcare is needed.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Schmidt, Kathrine Greby","Lerche, Anders Fritz","Christensen, Marie Raunkjær","Rasmussen, Charlotte Lund","Straker, Leon","Mathiassen, Svend Erik","Holtermann, Andreas"],"cuid":["S02-099.93-769569"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Schmidt, Kathrine Greby"],"country":["Australia","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Curtin University","University of Gävle","University of Gävle, Gävle, Sweden."],"keyword":["Child","Child care","Exercise physiology","Fatigue","Fra forskning til praksis","High intensity physical activity","Humans","Interventioner","Pain","Physical activity","RCT","Sedentary Behavior","Sundhedsmiljø","Workplace intervention"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effectiveness of a goldilocks work intervention in childcare workers a cluster randomized controlled trial","src":["nfa","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5271/sjweh.4145","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.93-769569","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11057501","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85189192259","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38436676","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["nfa-b0f4e982-525a-40c8-a1f4-0e670b9d8e61","sdu-5de39ee9-6c6f-4a65-860f-f7edd711629b"],"keyword":["Child","Child Care","Exercise/physiology","Fatigue","Fra forskning til praksis","Humans","Interventioner","Pain","RCT","Sedentary Behavior","Sundhedsmiljø","high-intensity physical activity","physical activity","workplace intervention"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-BB10C14181DBB631D998A3FB4EEF701E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/259707693/197_207_schmidt.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5271/sjweh.4145","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"nfa national research centre for the working environment","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-WORK","l3nn":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","namevar":["The National Research Centre for the Working Environment","The National Research Centre for the Working Environment, Directors, Musculoskeletal Disorders and Physical Workload"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-WORK","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, Research Unit of Physical Activity and Health in Working life"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"au","key":"curtin university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Curtin University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"university of gävle","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gävle","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"university of gävle gävle sweden","name":"University of Gävle, Gävle, Sweden.","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kathrine Greby","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"fe5a3b12-fb63-489c-845f-6c39c5e3d62f","name":"Schmidt, Kathrine Greby","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Fritz","key":"lerchepau","last":"Lerche","locid":"8ca85f92-8c87-439e-86fc-e2ca9cd5bbcb","name":"Lerche, Anders Fritz","orcid":"0000-0002-3199-6571","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie Raunkjær","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"73eadaa4-47b9-4eab-9dd0-1df7a2858a33","name":"Christensen, Marie Raunkjær","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte Lund","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Charlotte Lund","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"strakerpau","last":"Straker","name":"Straker, Leon","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Svend Erik","key":"mathiassenpau","last":"Mathiassen","name":"Mathiassen, Svend Erik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andreas","key":"holtermannpau","last":"Holtermann","locid":"f0d131d6-aefa-4dd3-a64e-2e237f273adf","name":"Holtermann, Andreas","orcid":"0000-0003-4825-5697","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38436676"],"pub":{"doi":"10.5271/sjweh.4145","issn":["03553140","03566528"],"issn_org":["03553140"],"issue":"3","journal":"Scandinavian Journal of Work, Environment & Health","pages":"197-207","publisher":"Nordic Association of Occupational Safety and Health (NOROSH)","status":"p","title":"Scandinavian Journal of Work, Environment & Health","volume":"50"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85189192259"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["nfa-b0f4e982-525a-40c8-a1f4-0e670b9d8e61","sdu-5de39ee9-6c6f-4a65-860f-f7edd711629b"],"src":["NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Effectiveness of a Goldilocks Work intervention in childcare workers – A cluster-randomized controlled trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.93-769569","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Introduction Intraventricular haemorrhage (IVH) is associated with high morbidity and mortality. External ventricular drainage (EVD) has been shown to decrease mortality. Although EVD is widely used, outcome and complication rates in EVD-treated patients with IVH are not fully elucidated. This study aims to describe EVD complication rates and outcomes in patients with primary and secondary IVH at two university hospitals in Denmark. The study will provide a historical reference of relevant endpoints for use in future clinical trials involving patients with IVH. Methods and analysis This descriptive, multicentre registry study included adult patients (age 18+) with primary or secondary IVH and treated with at least one EVD between 2017 and 2021 at Aarhus University Hospital or Odense University Hospital. Patients are identified using the Danish National Patient Register. Data are collected and recorded from patient medical records. Relevant descriptive statistics and correlation analyses will be applied. Ethics and dissemination Ethical approval and authorisation to access, store and analyse data have been obtained (Central Denmark Region Committee on Health Research Ethics). The research lead will present the results of the study. Data will be reported according to the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology and results submitted for publication in peer-reviewed journals.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Gu, Chenghao","Haldrup, Mette","Rasmussen, Mads","Dyrskog, Stig Eric","Simonsen, Claus Ziegler","Grønhøj, Mads Hjortdal","Poulsen, Frantz Rom","Busse, Thor","Wismann, Joakim","Debrabant, Birgit","Korshoej, Anders Rosendal"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-716158"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Korshoej, Anders Rosendal"],"keyword":["Adult","Adult intensive critical care","Cerebral Hemorrhage complications","Denmark epidemiology","Drainage adverse effects","Humans","Multicenter Studies as Topic","Neurosurgery","Registries","Stroke","Treatment Outcome"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"descriptive registry study on outcome and complications of external ventricular drainage treatment of intraventricular haemorrhage in a danish cohort a study protocol","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2023-075997","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-716158","identifier":[{"id":"38238178","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10806758","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85182840622","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-f4bf4eb6-6469-431b-a21a-d6b04172fdf9","sdu-c9cbabd4-8f1c-40c0-80a3-ed17f7363eb3"],"keyword":["Adult","Adult intensive & critical care","Cerebral Hemorrhage/complications","Denmark/epidemiology","Drainage/adverse effects","Humans","Multicenter Studies as Topic","NEUROSURGERY","Registries","Stroke","Treatment Outcome"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-B290665892794B4CBF8AFC0E6F0DBF22"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2023-075997","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/253704757/e075997.full.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Neurosurgery"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Neurology (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Odense University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Chenghao","key":"gupau","last":"Gu","name":"Gu, Chenghao","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette","key":"haldruppau","last":"Haldrup","locid":"d74af99e-75ee-4bac-a163-419689c2ca5e","name":"Haldrup, Mette","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"10307123-564d-4db1-872d-d0a91168ce4e","name":"Rasmussen, Mads","orcid":"0000-0002-0416-6767","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stig","key":"dyrskogpau","last":"Dyrskog","locid":"ceaf7914-5286-4f5c-b11e-1be0fce5aa41","name":"Dyrskog, Stig Eric","name_org":"Dyrskog, Stig","orcid":"0000-0002-1903-1742","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Claus Ziegler","key":"simonsenpau","last":"Simonsen","locid":"ccf2fb52-6a4a-45b2-adb0-d6e83324879f","name":"Simonsen, Claus Ziegler","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Mads Hjortdal","key":"grønhøjpau","last":"Grønhøj","locid":"c3b774fa-6d98-40ae-8ee3-514bde5a9170","name":"Grønhøj, Mads Hjortdal","orcid":"0000-0002-1473-9872","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Frantz Rom","key":"poulsenpau","last":"Poulsen","locid":"b50645f6-f0c4-4356-9f63-d44290b0dfd9","name":"Poulsen, Frantz Rom","orcid":"0000-0001-5715-6901","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Thor","key":"bussepau","last":"Busse","locid":"d1175519-4526-4624-8d0a-d307d956666c","name":"Busse, Thor Löwe","name_org":"Busse, Thor","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Joakim","key":"wismannpau","last":"Wismann","locid":"2e305d8f-96e2-45d0-8694-9968a488acdf","name":"Wismann, Joakim","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Birgit","key":"debrabantpau","last":"Debrabant","locid":"bc47d3aa-9760-4139-9a72-ae553777b623","name":"Debrabant, Birgit","orcid":"0000-0002-1964-3204","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders Rosendal","key":"korshoejpau","last":"Korshoej","locid":"09d94078-676a-42ee-91c2-eb8ba8df356b","name":"Korshoej, Anders Rosendal","orcid":"0000-0003-4285-8171","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38238178"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1136/bmjopen-2023-075997","issn":["20446055"],"issue":"1","journal":"BMJ Open","paperid":"e075997","publisher":"BMJ Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"BMJ Open","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85182840622"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-f4bf4eb6-6469-431b-a21a-d6b04172fdf9","sdu-c9cbabd4-8f1c-40c0-80a3-ed17f7363eb3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Descriptive registry study on outcome and complications of external ventricular drainage treatment of intraventricular haemorrhage in a Danish cohort : A study protocol","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-716158","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Objective. This study aimed to examine differences in heart rate variability (HRV) across accelerometer-derived position, self-reported sleep, and different summary measures (sleep, 24 h HRV) in free-living settings using open-source methodology. Approach. HRV is a biomarker of autonomic activity. As it is strongly affected by factors such as physical behaviour, stress, and sleep, ambulatory HRV analysis is challenging. Beat-to-beat heart rate (HR) and accelerometry data were collected using single-lead electrocardiography and trunk- and thigh-worn accelerometers among 160 adults participating in the SCREENS trial. HR files were processed and analysed in the RHRV R package. Start time and duration spent in physical behaviours were extracted, and time and frequency analysis for each episode was performed. Differences in HRV estimates across activities were compared using linear mixed models adjusted for age and sex with subject ID as random effect. Next, repeated-measures Bland-Altman analysis was used to compare 24 h RMSSD estimates to HRV during self-reported sleep. Sensitivity analyses evaluated the accuracy of the methodology, and the approach of employing accelerometer-determined episodes to examine activity-independent HRV was described. Main results. HRV was estimated for 31 289 episodes in 160 individuals (53.1% female) at a mean age of 41.4 years. Significant differences in HR and most markers of HRV were found across positions [Mean differences RMSSD: Sitting (Reference) − Standing (−2.63 ms) or Lying (4.53 ms)]. Moreover, ambulatory HRV differed significantly across sleep status, and poor agreement between 24 h estimates compared to sleep HRV was detected. Sensitivity analyses confirmed that removing the first and last 30 s of accelerometry-determined HR episodes was an accurate strategy to account for orthostatic effects. Significance. Ambulatory HRV differed significantly across accelerometry-assigned positions and sleep. The proposed approach for free-living HRV analysis may be an effective strategy to remove confounding by physical activity when the aim is to monitor general autonomic stress.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Rietz, Marlene","Pedersen, Jesper","Rasmussen, Martin Gillies Banke","Sørensen, Sarah Overgaard","Mortensen, Sofie Rath","Brage, Søren","Kristensen, Peter Lund","Grøntved, Anders","Brønd, Jan Christian"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-793604"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Brønd, Jan Christian"],"country":["Sweden","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska Institute","University of Cambridge"],"keyword":["Accelerometry","Behaviour","Free living","Heart rate variability"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-KP"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-KP"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"facilitating ambulatory heart rate variability analysis using accelerometry based classifications of body position and self reported sleep","src":["kp","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-KP"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6579/ad450d","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The SCREENS project was funded by the European Research Council (Grant Number 716657). We would like to thank and acknowledge all individuals which participated in the SCREENS RCT. Moreover, thank you to research assistant Jasmin Helledie, who assisted during data collection, and to the researcher service organization Open Patient Data Explorative Network, Odense, Denmark. Finally, we\\u2019d like to thank Line G Olesen for her past engagement in the project."],"id":"S02-100.00-793604","identifier":[{"id":"85194075158","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38684167","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["kp-207490ba-257b-4d77-ae7e-adbccafcb24f","sdu-b651c994-29dd-4d44-ade7-df657fdb79db"],"keyword":["RHRV","accelerometry","behaviour","free-living","heart rate variability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-8803ABCDD762652F411416FDB071AFBF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/264327199/Rietz_2024_Physiol._Meas._45_055016.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6579/ad450d","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, RICH - Research In Childhood Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"karolinska institute","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institute","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"kp university college copenhagen","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-KP","l3nl":"KP","l3nn":"KP University College Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KP University College Copenhagen","namevar":["University College Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-KP","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Næstved-Slagelse-Ringsted Hospitals"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"university of cambridge","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Cambridge","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marlene","key":"rietzpau","last":"Rietz","locid":"11707e1a-a853-411c-a38c-b10a6c65914b","name":"Rietz, Marlene","orcid":"0000-0002-6422-6215","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"schmidtperssonpau","last":"Schmidt-Persson","locid":"f321394d-8abe-49b2-ab02-1b528e734f63","name":"Pedersen, Jesper","name_org":"Schmidt-Persson, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-9475-0341","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"gilliesbankerasmussenpau","last":"Gillies Banke Rasmussen","locid":"8b86a1ef-e6c1-4de3-8300-e30d144bbf60","name":"Rasmussen, Martin Gillies Banke","name_org":"Gillies Banke Rasmussen, Martin","orcid":"0000-0002-2114-2185","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sarah","key":"overgaardsørensenpau","last":"Overgaard Sørensen","locid":"ee7fa7d0-8bb4-4eca-a31d-26ce06940514","name":"Sørensen, Sarah Overgaard","name_org":"Overgaard Sørensen, Sarah","orcid":"0000-0002-3953-3387","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Sofie","key":"rathmortensenpau","last":"Rath Mortensen","locid":"ce405612-8eed-42e1-9ba3-63ca26241605","name":"Mortensen, Sofie Rath","name_org":"Rath Mortensen, Sofie","orcid":"0000-0002-9550-1507","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren","key":"bragepau","last":"Brage","locid":"5dc5eeb6-2ba6-4478-9111-eb87dc9d54d9","name":"Brage, Søren","orcid":"0000-0002-1265-7355","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"lundkristensenpau","last":"Lund Kristensen","locid":"751d26de-66d4-4ef9-8183-880c48c96dc7","name":"Kristensen, Peter Lund","name_org":"Lund Kristensen, Peter","orcid":"0000-0002-5666-9614","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"grøntvedpau","last":"Grøntved","locid":"393b1ca1-e658-437c-9ca3-73aea925ad58","name":"Grøntved, Anders","orcid":"0000-0003-1584-679X","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan Christian","key":"brøndpau","last":"Brønd","locid":"da4ecea2-2b7f-4027-9859-416024dca432","name":"Brønd, Jan Christian","orcid":"0000-0001-6718-3022","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38684167"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1088/1361-6579/ad450d","issn":["09517979","09673334"],"issn_org":["09673334"],"issue":"5","journal":"Physiological Measurement","pages":"1-17","paperid":"055016","publisher":"IOP Publishing","status":"p","title":"Physiological Measurement","volume":"45"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85194075158"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","Hospitals","KP","Region Zealand","Universities","University Colleges","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["kp-207490ba-257b-4d77-ae7e-adbccafcb24f","sdu-b651c994-29dd-4d44-ade7-df657fdb79db"],"src":["KP University College Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Facilitating ambulatory heart rate variability analysis using accelerometry-based classifications of body position and self-reported sleep","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-793604","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: Vitamin D deficiency in pregnancy may increase the risk of autism and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD). Objective: The objective of this study was to estimate the effect of vitamin D3 supplementation in pregnancy on risk of autism and ADHD. Design: This randomized clinical trial was part of the COpenhagen Prospective Study on Neuro-PSYCHiatric Development (COPYCH) project nested within the Copenhagen Prospective Studies on Asthma in Childhood 2010 (COPSAC2010) cohort comprising a population-based sample of 700 healthy mother-child pairs enrolled at week 24 of pregnancy. Maternal 25-hydroxy-vitamin D (25(OH)D) was measured at inclusion and 623 mothers were randomized 1:1 to either high-dose (2800 IU/d) or standard dose (400 IU/d) vitamin D3 until 1 wk postpartum (315 received high-dose, 308 standard dose). At age 10, diagnoses and symptom load of autism and ADHD, respectively, were established using the Kiddie-Schedule for Affective Disorders and Schizophrenia for School-Age Children-Present and Lifetime Version. Results: The psychopathologic evaluation was completed by 591 children aged 10 y, and 16 children (2.7%) were diagnosed with autism and 65 (11.0%) with ADHD. Hereof, 496 children participated in the vitamin D3 trial (246 received high-dose, 250 standard dose). Of these, 12 children (2.4%) were diagnosed with autism and 58 (11.7%) with ADHD. Higher maternal preintervention 25(OH)D levels were associated with a decreased risk of autism [odd ratio (OR) per 10 nmol/L: 0.76 (0.59,0.97); P = 0.034], lower autistic symptom load [β per 10 nmol/L: −0.03 (−0.05,0.00); P = 0.024), and decreased risk of ADHD diagnosis (OR per 10 nmol/L: 0.88 (0.78,0.99); P = 0.033]. High-dose vitamin D3 supplementation was not associated with risk of autism or ADHD. Conclusions: Higher maternal preintervention 25(OH)D was associated with a decreased risk of autism, lower autistic symptom load, and decreased risk of ADHD diagnosis, but high-dose vitamin D3 supplementation in pregnancy had no effect on risk of autism and ADHD. This trial was registered at clinicaltrials.gov as NCT00856947.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Aagaard, Kristina","Jepsen, Jens Richardt Møllegaard","Sevelsted, Astrid","Horner, David","Vinding, Rebecca","Rosenberg, Julie Bøjstrup","Brustad, Nicklas","Eliasen, Anders","Mohammadzadeh, Parisa","Følsgaard, Nilofar","Hernández-lorca, María","Fagerlund, Birgitte","Glenthøj, Birte","Rasmussen, Morten Arendt","Bilenberg, Niels","Stokholm, Jakob","Bønnelykke, Klaus","Ebdrup, Bjørn H.","Chawes, Bo"],"cuid":["S03-098.50-725020"],"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Chawes, Bo"],"keyword":["ADHD","Autism","Neurodevelopment","Supplementation","Vitamin D"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"high dose vitamin d000000000003 supplementation in pregnancy and risk of neurodevelopmental disorders in the children at age 000000000010 a randomized clinical trial","src":["dtu","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajcnut.2023.12.002","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["All funding received by COPSAC is listed on www.copsac.com. The Lundbeck Foundation (Grant no R16-A1694); The Ministry of Health (Grant no 903516); Danish Council for Strategic Research (Grant no 0603-00280B) and The Capital Region Research Foundation have provided core support to the COPSAC research centre. This project has received funding from the European Research Council (ERC) under the European Union's Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme (grant agreement No. 946228) (BC). BHE is part of the Advisory Board of Eli Lilly Denmark A/S, Janssen-Cilag, Lundbeck Pharma A/S, and Takeda Pharmaceutical Company Ltd; and has received lecture fees from Bristol-Myers Squibb, Boehringer Ingelheim, Otsuka Pharma Scandinavia AB, Eli Lilly Company, and Lundbeck Pharma A/S. BG has been the leader of a Lundbeck Foundation Centre of Excellence for Clinical Intervention and Neuropsychiatric Schizophrenia Research (CINS) (January 2009 – December 2021), which was partially financed by an independent grant from the Lundbeck Foundation based on international review and partially financed by the Mental Health Services in the Capital Region of Denmark, the University of Copenhagen, and other foundations. All grants are the property of the Mental Health Services in the Capital Region of Denmark and administrated by them. She has no other conflicts to disclose. All other authors report no conflicts of interest. The funding agencies did not have any role in design and conduct of the study; collection, management, and interpretation of the data; or preparation, review, or approval of the manuscript. No pharmaceutical company was involved in the study. All funding received by COPSAC is listed on www.copsac.com. The Lundbeck Foundation (Grant no R16-A1694 ); The Ministry of Health (Grant no 903516 ); Danish Council for Strategic Research (Grant no 0603-00280B ) and The Capital Region Research Foundation have provided core support to the COPSAC research centre. This project has received funding from the European Research Council (ERC) under the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme (grant agreement No. 946228 ) (BC)."],"id":"S03-098.50-725020","identifier":[{"id":"85182884241","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38072183","type":"pmid"},{"id":"001170404900001","type":"wos"}],"ids":["dtu-3345464b-ffb9-4a21-8001-7597d2c76f5d","ku-cb015b60-4c32-4170-b369-176268e405cb","sdu-