{"abstract":"A polyomino is a polygonal region with axis-parallel edges and corners of integral coordinates, which may have holes. In this paper, we consider planar tiling and packing problems with polyomino pieces and a polyomino container P. We give polynomial-time algorithms for deciding if P can be tiled with k × k squares for any fixed k which can be part of the input (that is, deciding if P is the union of a set of non-overlapping k × k squares) and for packing P with a maximum number of non-overlapping and axis-parallel 2 × 1 dominos, allowing rotations by 90°. As packing is more general than tiling, the latter algorithm can also be used to decide if P can be tiled by 2 × 1 dominos.These are classical problems with important applications in VLSI design, and the related problem of finding a maximum packing of 2 × 2 squares is known to be NP-hard [6]. For our three problems there are known pseudo-polynomial-time algorithms, that is, algorithms with running times polynomial in the area or perimeter of P. However, the standard, compact way to represent a polygon is by listing the coordinates of the corners in binary. We use this representation, and thus present the first polynomial-time algorithms for the problems. Concretely, we give a simple O(n log n)-time algorithm for tiling with squares, where n is the number of corners of P. We then give a more involved algorithm that reduces the problems of packing and tiling with dominos to finding a maximum and perfect matching in a graph with O(n3) vertices. This leads to algorithms with running times and , respectively.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Aamand, Anders","Abrahamsen, Mikkel","Rasmussen, Peter Michael Reichstein","Ahle, Thomas D."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-732690"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Massachusetts Institute of Technology","Meta"],"keyword":["Packing","Tiling","polyominos"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"tiling with squares and packing dominos in polynomial time","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3597932","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-732690","identifier":[{"id":"85166390685","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-e485b67e-19c9-49f1-b41b-c897d9e0650a"],"keyword":["Packing","polyominos","tiling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B885A44ED1AC9A31450312B856939716"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/384025123/Tiling_with_Squares.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3597932","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"massachusetts institute of technology","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Algorithms and Complexity"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"meta","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Meta","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Anders","key":"aamandpau","last":"Aamand","name":"Aamand, Anders","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Mikkel","key":"abra