eral Long-Term Registry","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-106086","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Understanding how niche-based and neutral processes contribute to the spatial variation in plant–pollinator interactions is central to designing effective pollination conservation schemes. Such schemes are needed to reverse declines of wild bees and other pollinating insects, and to promote pollination services to wild and cultivated plants. We used data on wild bee interactions with plants belonging to the four tribes Loteae, Trifolieae, Anthemideae and either spring- or summer-flowering Cichorieae, sampled systematically along a 682 km latitudinal gradient to build models that allowed us to 1) predict occurrences of pairwise bee–flower interactions across 115 sampling locations, and 2) estimate the contribution of variables hypothesized to be related to niche-based assembly structuring processes (viz. annual mean temperature, landscape diversity, bee sociality, bee phenology and flower preferences of bees) and neutral processes (viz. regional commonness and dispersal distance to conspecifics). While neutral processes were important predictors of plant–pollinator distributions, niche-based processes were reflected in the contrasting distributions of solitary bee and bumble bees along the temperature gradient, and in the influence of bee flower preferences on the distribution of bee species across plant types. In particular, bee flower preferences separated bees into three main groups, albeit with some overlap: visitors to spring-flowering Cichorieae; visitors to Anthemideae and summer-flowering Cichorieae; and visitors to Trifolieae and Loteae. Our findings suggest that both neutral and niche-based processes are significant contributors to the spatial distribution of plant–pollinator interactions so that conservation actions in our region should be directed towards areas: near high concentrations of known occurrences of regionally rare bees; in mild climatic conditions; and that are surrounded by heterogenous landscapes. Given the observed niche-based differences, the proportion of functionally distinct plants in flower-mixes could be chosen to target bee species, or guilds, of conservation concern.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Sydenham, Markus Arne Kjær","Venter, Zander S.","R. Moe, Stein","Eldegard, Katrine","Kuhlmann, Michael","Reitan, Trond","Rasmussen, Claus","Paxton, Robert J","Dupont, Yoko Luise","Skrindo, Astrid B.","Hegland, Stein Joar","Nielsen, Anders","Olesen, Jens M.","S. Nowell, Megan","Rusch, Graciela M."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-194130"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Sydenham, Markus Arne Kjær"],"country":["Germany","Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","German Centre for Integrative Biodiversity Research (iDiv)","Kiel University","Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg","Norwegian Institute for Nature Research","Norwegian Institute of Bioeconomy Research","Norwegian University of Life Sciences","University of Oslo","Western Norway University of Applied Sciences"],"keyword":["Ecological networks","Machine learning","Plant pollinator interactions","Spatial","Wild bees"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"neutral processes related to regional bee commonness and dispersal distances are important predictors of plant pollinator networks along gradients of climate and landscape conditions","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/ecog.06379","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-194130","identifier":[{"id":"85139937582","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-7177b320-cd61-4809-94bc-e43404262945"],"keyword":["ecological networks","machine learning","plant–pollinator interactions","spatial","wild bees"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A06C3802FE72804925C46C06068D58CD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/ecog.06379","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"no","key":"norwegian institute for nature research","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Norwegian Institute for Nature Research","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"norwegian university of life sciences","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Norwegian University of Life Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"kiel university","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Kiel University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"no","key":"university of oslo","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Oslo","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Agroecology, Department of Agroecology - Plant Pathology and Microbiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"de","key":"martin luther university halle wittenberg","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Martin Luther University Halle-Wittenberg","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"de","key":"german centre for integrative biodiversity research","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"German Centre for Integrative Biodiversity Research (iDiv)","used":1},{"affno":"8","country":"no","key":"western norway university of applied sciences","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Western Norway Univer