:"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-fae1fc17-f73b-4af2-b42d-71f4d759d7b5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Transmission of SARS-CoV-2 Omicron VOC subvariants BA.1 and BA.2 : Evidence from Danish Households","type":"Other-Unknown","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-543184","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Anders Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-593554"],"filters":{"added":"2022-07","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Colonialism","Diplomacy","Empire"],"level":"pop","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the u s having territories perpetuates inequality and colonialism nothing has changed in more than a century","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/outlook/2022/06/06/us-having-territories-perpetuates-inequality-colonialism/","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-593554","ids":["sdu-46e562f8-506c-406a-bb95-66754ab9a4f1"],"keyword":["Colonialism","Empire","SCOTUS","diplomacy"],"lang":"English","level":"Popular","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-083E7CE8648B6848EC8222AB6380BA61"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/outlook/2022/06/06/us-having-territories-perpetuates-inequality-colonialism/","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Department of History","SDU, Faculty of Humanities, Department of History, Center for American Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders Bo","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"f284f3a3-297f-4882-81e1-b591c41ead80","name":"Rasmussen, Anders Bo","orcid":"0000-0001-9817-9595","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["sdu-46e562f8-506c-406a-bb95-66754ab9a4f1"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The U.S. having territories perpetuates inequality and colonialism : Nothing has changed in more than a century","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-593554","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background: Singing for Lung Health (SLH) has recently been found effective in improving Six-Minute Walking Test Distance (6MWD) and quality of life (St. George’s Respiratory Questionnaire (SGRQ Total Score)) in an RCT within 10 weeks’ pulmonary rehabilitation (PR) [1]. Previous studies suggest that singing is beneficial for lung function, breathing control, and dyspnoea. However, the body-of-evidence is scarse with contradictory findings. Methods: Post-hoc analyses in per-protocol population, exploring physiological parameters in SLH: Lung function, inspiratory muscle strength and control, dyspnoea, and exercise-induced changes in pulse and saturation. Analyses: Baseline characteristics, stratified analyses, and logistic regression. Results: In total, 108 received SLH. Baseline caharcteristics in the SLH group were: Females: 56.5%; mean age 70.5 ±8.4 years; pack years 40.6 ±21.9; BMI: 28.3 ±5.8; and FEV1% predicted: 51.4 ±16.8. 31 (29%) achieved minimal important difference (MID) in 6MWD (≥30 m), which was associated with improved heart rate response (OR: 3.14; p=0.03). SGRQ MID (≥4 points) was achieved by 53 (49%) and was associated with improved maximal inspiratory pressure (OR: 4.35; p=0.04). We found no associations with spirometric changes. Conclusions: SLH seems to provide relevant physiological changes in COPD. Further studies are needed to confirm short-term and explore long-term findings. 1. Use of Singing for Lung Health as an alternative training modality within pulmonary rehabilitation for COPD: an RCT. M. Kaasgaard, D. Bech Rasmussen, K. Andreasson, A. Løkke, P. Vuust, O. Hilberg, U. Bødtger. European Respiratory Journal 2021. DOI: 10.1183/13993003.01142-2021 1 Use of Singing for Lung Health as an alternative training modality within pulmonary rehabilitation for COPD: an RCT. M. Kaasgaard, D. Bech Rasmussen, K. Andreasson, A. Løkke, P. Vuust, O. Hilberg, U. Bødtger. European Respiratory Journal 2021. DOI: 10.1183/13993003.01142-2021","author_count":6,"contributors":["Kaasgaard, Mette","Rasmussen, Daniel Bech","Løkke, Anders","Vuust, Peter","Hilberg, Ole","Bodtger, Uffe"],"cuid":["S02-095.45-793377"],"event":{"end":"2022-09-06","place":"Barcelona, es","start":"2022-09-04","title":"European Respiratory Society International Congress 2022","year":"2022"},"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sing a lung does singing for lung health provide physiological changes in copd","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.congress-2022.372","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-095.45-793377","ids":["au-a3d2c05d-fc31-499f-b35e-bdfc57167759","sdu-59626a99-8039-4493-b7f3-b2ae810c2fc9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-DFCB9F1B8783594E01BDEED98925260D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.congress-2022.372","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Center for Music In the Brain"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Zealand University Hospital, Roskilde"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital, Research Unit of Medicine (Vejle)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mette","key":"kaasgaardpau","last":"Kaasgaard","locid":"fc5784b6-66d8-4b92-8c4a-74bf4628c9c3","name":"Kaasgaard, Mette","orcid":"0000-0003-3833-2943","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"vuustpau","last":"Vuust","locid":"2c697ab6-9b06-4a16-a687-c8cdbd56f661","name":"Vuust, Peter","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel Bech","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Daniel Bech","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Løkke","key":"ottesenpau","last":"Ottesen","locid":"db3d3870-0459-4783-8f0b-6a8e5d9dce78","name":"Løkke, Anders","name_org":"Ottesen, Anders Løkke","orcid":"0000-0002-6905-4141","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ole","key":"hilbergpau","last":"Hilberg","locid":"6c545c65-1d94-45a3-a8b4-f90eae06d248","name":"Hilberg, Ole","orcid":"0000-0002-3075-3463","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Uffe","key":"bodtgerpau","last":"Bodtger","locid":"2f4e70da-63bb-4b8d-af09-2a959d6df5e8","name":"Bodtger, Uffe","orcid":"0000-0002-1231-9209","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1183/13993003.congress-2022.372","issn":["09031936","13993003"],"issn_org":["09031936"],"issue":"Suppl. 66","journal":"European Respiratory Journal","paperid":"372","publisher":"European Respiratory Society","status":"p","title":"European Respiratory Journal","volume":"60"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-a3d2c05d-fc31-499f-b35e-bdfc57167759","sdu-59626a99-8039-4493-b7f3-b2ae810c2fc9"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Sing-a-Lung: Does Singing for Lung Health provide physiological changes in COPD?","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.45-793377","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Screen media use is part of most children's everyday lives, but organisations have advised that use should be limited. The aims of this study were to describe 6-11-year-old Danish children's screen device ownership and screen media use (weekdays and weekends), including the role of parental education, family structure and household screen media rules. We conducted a cross-sectional study including 5274 Danish children aged 6-11-years sampled from ten Danish municipalities from May 2019 to November 2020. Characteristics of the sample and source population were obtained from the Danish Health Data Authority. Parent's completed the SCREENS questionnaire, which was developed to assess children's screen media habits. We used inverse probability weighted logistic and linear regression models. Smartphone and laptop ownership was higher with increasing age, and use of screen media varied across day type, age and gender. The proportion of children using screen media more than 4 h/day was 13% (95% CI 12%;14%) for weekdays and 28% (95% CI 27%;29%) for weekend days. Children of parents with medium-length or long educations had statistically significant lower odds of using screen media more than 4 h/day. We found a statistically significant graded relationship between household screen media rules and children's screen media use; the less parents reported presence of rules, the more time their children spent on screen media engagements. Our results suggest that parental educational level and family structure are related to unfavourable screen media habits, and household screen media rules may play an important role for parents to limit children's screen use.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Pedersen, Jesper","Rasmussen, Martin Gillies Banke","Olesen, Line Grønholt","Klakk, Heidi","Kristensen, Peter Lund","Grøntved, Anders"],"cuid":["S02-096.19-048828"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Grøntved, Anders"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCL","DI-UC-UCS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCL","DI-UC-UCS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"recreational screen media use in danish school aged children and the role of parental education family structures and household screen media rules","src":["sdu","ucsyd"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ypmed.2021.106908","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-096.19-048828","identifier":[{"id":"85121676317","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34915040","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-40db67b9-c29c-45dd-b292-e75d83d3d431","ucsyd-433955c4-e252-4dfe-a141-a049643047aa"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-EC0E4FA4D283274DCFE9C0F8FB2FAD4A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/196594493/1_s2.0_S0091743521004813_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ypmed.2021.106908","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, RICH - Research In Childhood Health","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, Research Unit of Exercise Epidemiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ucl university college","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-UCL","l3nl":"UCL University College","l3nn":"UCL University College","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"UCL University College","namevar":["University College Lillebælt"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-UCL","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"uc syd university college south denmark","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-UCS","l3nl":"UC SYD, University College South Denmark","l3nn":"UC SYD University College South Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"UC SYD University College South Denmark","namevar":["UC SYD, Pædagogik og skole, Forskning, Forskning"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-UCS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"f321394d-8abe-49b2-ab02-1b528e734f63","name":"Pedersen, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0002-9475-0341","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin Gillies","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"8b86a1ef-e6c1-4de3-8300-e30d144bbf60","name":"Rasmussen, Martin Gillies Banke","name_org":"Rasmussen, Martin Gillies","orcid":"0000-0002-2114-2185","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line Grønholt","key":"olesenpau","last":"Olesen","locid":"e9ad7180-8cce-4e88-9707-18dedc4c73e0","name":"Olesen, Line Grønholt","orcid":"0000-0002-6588-8878","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Heidi","key":"klakkpau","last":"Klakk","locid":"962412f0-020a-41bf-9b4b-47f926964062","name":"Klakk, Heidi","orcid":"0000-0002-9299-4334","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Heidi Klakk","key":"egebækpau","last":"Egebæk","locid":"0ca52676-95ab-44b8-89f9-0dd375d8adb8","name":"Klakk, Heidi","name_org":"Egebæk, Heidi Klakk","orcid":"0000-0002-9299-4334","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter Lund","key":"kristensenpau","last":"Kristensen","locid":"751d26de-66d4-4ef9-8183-880c48c96dc7","name":"Kristensen, Peter Lund","orcid":"0000-0002-5666-9614","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"grøntvedpau","last":"Grøntved","locid":"393b1ca1-e658-437c-9ca3-73aea925ad58","name":"Grøntved, Anders","orcid":"0000-0003-1584-679X","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34915040"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ypmed.2021.106908","issn":["00917435","10960260"],"issn_org":["00917435"],"journal":"Preventive Medicine","paperid":"106908","publisher":"Academic Press Inc.","status":"p","title":"Preventive Medicine","volume":"155"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85121676317"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","UC SYD, University College South Denmark","UCL University College","Universities","University Colleges","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-40db67b9-c29c-45dd-b292-e75d83d3d431","ucsyd-433955c4-e252-4dfe-a141-a049643047aa"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark","UC SYD University College South Denmark"],"title":"Recreational screen media use in Danish school-aged children and the role of parental education, family structures, and household screen media rules","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.19-048828","year":"2022","yearsub":2023}
{"abstract":"Background: Patients admitted to an intensive care unit (ICU) with active haematological malignancy and hypoxaemic respiratory failure have a high mortality. Oxygen supplementation is essential, but limited information exists on the optimum oxygenation targets in these patients. Methods: This subgroup analysis was specified before completion of the Handling Oxygenation Targets in the ICU (HOT-ICU) trial. The trial investigated the effects of a lower (8 kPa) vs a higher (12 kPa) arterial oxygenation target and was stratified for active haematological malignancy, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and site. We here report the primary outcome (90-day mortality) and selected secondary outcomes in the subgroup of patients with active haematological malignancy. Results: The HOT-ICU trial included 168 patients with active haematological malignancy; 82 were randomly allocated to an arterial oxygenation target of 8 kPa, and 86 to 12 kPa. At 90 days, 53/81 patients (65%) in the lower-oxygenation group and 47/86 patients (55%) in the higher-oxygenation group had died: adjusted relative risk 1.22 (95% confidence interval 0.95–1.56); at 1 year, the numbers were 58/81 (72%) vs 56/86 (65%): adjusted relative risk 1.11 (95% confidence interval 0.90–1.36). No statistically significant differences were found for any secondary outcomes. Conclusion: In ICU patients with active haematological malignancies and hypoxaemic respiratory failure, we found a high mortality at 90 days and 1 year. Our results did not preclude clinically relevant benefits or harms of a lower oxygenation target in patients with active haematological malignancy. A randomised trial may, therefore, be worthwhile for these patients. Clinical trial registration: NCT03174002.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Lass Klitgaard, Thomas","Schjørring, Olav Lilleholt","Severinsen, Marianne Tang","Perner, Anders","Rasmussen, Bodil Steen"],"cuid":["S02-097.52-792881"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Critical care","Haematologic neoplasms","Hyperoxia","Hypoxia","Oxygen Inhalation Therapy"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"lower versus higher oxygenation targets in icu patients with haematological malignancy insights from the hot icu trial","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bjao.2022.100090","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-097.52-792881","identifier":[{"id":"85181223799","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-60cf4ce0-6551-45bc-a6dc-b76c00a01b7a","ku-d63912e7-6936-42f7-ba7e-ee52e4b07069"],"keyword":["Critical care","Haematologic neoplasms","Hyperoxia","Hypoxia","Oxygen inhalation therapy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-1689A6EBB3171FDE6D58243DAF72E62A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bjao.2022.100090","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/505824035/Klitgaard_et_al_82022_Lower_versus_higher_oxygenation_targets_in_ICU_patients_with_haematological_malignancy_insights_from_the_HOT_ICU_trial.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/424033391/1_s2.0_S2772609622000892_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Department of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Klinik Anæstesi, Børn, Kredsløb og Kvinder","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Anaesthesiology","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"lassklitgaardpau","last":"Lass Klitgaard","locid":"f3e0e4b8-0772-4f4f-8be2-b99763b3917c","name":"Lass Klitgaard, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-8781-1206","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olav Lilleholt","key":"schjørringpau","last":"Schjørring","locid":"7dac619e-9b5c-46ac-9170-10ac13165da5","name":"Schjørring, Olav Lilleholt","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Marianne Tang","key":"severinsenpau","last":"Severinsen","locid":"fcac056d-4da6-4995-8375-2d5d5e30a2f8","name":"Severinsen, Marianne Tang","orcid":"0000-0003-0996-1812","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Anders","key":"pernerpau","last":"Perner","locid":"8a389541-d529-4ee3-8364-66bfc85f77c9","name":"Perner, Anders","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bodil Steen","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"3162c449-94c3-4d28-8dbb-c134d6eb76e1","name":"Rasmussen, Bodil Steen","orcid":"0000-0003-2190-145X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.bjao.2022.100090","issn":["27726096"],"journal":"BJA Open","paperid":"100090","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"BJA Open","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85181223799"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-60cf4ce0-6551-45bc-a6dc-b76c00a01b7a","ku-d63912e7-6936-42f7-ba7e-ee52e4b07069"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Lower versus higher oxygenation targets in ICU patients with haematological malignancy — insights from the HOT-ICU trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.52-792881","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: Gastrointestinal bleeding (GIB) risk in relation to concomitant treatment with non-vitamin K oral anticoagulants (NOAC) and oral glucocorticoids is insufficiently explored. We aimed to investigate the short-term risk following coexposure. METHODS: This is a register-based, nationwide Danish study including patients with atrial fibrillation on NOACs during 2012-2018. Patients were defined as exposed to oral glucocorticoids if they claimed a prescription within 60 days prior to GIB. We investigated the associations between GIB and oral glucocorticoid exposure, reporting HRs via a nested case-control design and absolute risk via a cohort design. Matching terms were age, sex, calendar year, follow-up time and NOAC agent. RESULTS: 98 376 patients on NOACs (median age: 75 years (IQR: 68-82), 44% female) were included, and 16% redeemed at least one oral glucocorticoid prescription within 3 years. HRs of GIB were increased comparing exposed with non-exposed patients (<20 mg daily dose, HR 1.54 (95% CI 1.29 to 1.84); ≥20 mg daily dose, HR 2.19 (95% CI 1.81 to 2.65)). 60-day standardised absolute risk of GIB following first claimed oral glucocorticoid prescription increased compared with non-exposed: 60-day absolute risk: 0.71% (95% CI 0.58% to 0.85%) vs 0.38% (95% CI 0.32% to 0.43%). The relative risk was elevated as well: risk ratio of 1.89 (95% CI 1.43 to 2.36). CONCLUSIONS: Concomitant treatment with NOACs and oral glucocorticoids was associated with a short-term rate and risk increase of GIB compared with patients only on NOACs. This could have implications for clinical management, necessitating closer monitoring or other risk mitigation strategies during episodes of cotreatment with oral glucocorticoids.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Holt, Anders","Blanche, Paul","Zareini, Bochra","Rasmussen, Peter Vibe","Strange, Jarl Emanuel","Rajan, Deepthi","Jensen, Mads Hashiba","El-Sheikh, Mohammed","Schjerning, Anne-Marie","Schou, Morten","Gislason, 