{"abstract":"African wild pigs have a contentious evolutionary and biogeographic history. Until recently, desert warthog (Phacochoerus aethiopicus) and common warthog (P. africanus) were considered a single species. Molecular evidence surprisingly suggested they diverged at least 4.4 million years ago, and possibly outside of Africa. We sequenced the first whole-genomes of four desert warthogs and 35 common warthogs from throughout their range. We show that these two species diverged much later than previously estimated, 400,000-1,700,000 years ago depending on assumptions of gene flow. This brings it into agreement with the paleontological record. We found that the common warthog originated in western Africa and subsequently colonized eastern and southern Africa. During this range expansion, the common warthog interbred with the desert warthog, presumably in eastern Africa, underlining this region's importance in African biogeography. We found that immune system-related genes may have adaptively introgressed into common warthogs, indicating that resistance to novel diseases was one of the most potent drivers of evolution as common warthogs expanded their range. Hence, we solve some of the key controversies surrounding warthog evolution and reveal a complex evolutionary history involving range expansion, introgression, and adaptation to new diseases.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Garcia-Erill, Genís","Jørgensen, Christian H. F.","Muwanika, Vincent B.","Wang, Xi","Rasmussen, Malthe Sebro","de Jong, Yvonne A.","Gaubert, Philippe","Olayemi, Ayodeji","Salmona, Jordi","Butynski, Thomas M.","Bertola, Laura D.","Siegismund, Hans Redlef","Albrechtsen, Anders","Heller, Rasmus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-431959"],"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Africa","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France","Kenya","Nigeria","Uganda"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Eastern Africa Primate Diversity and Conservation Program & Lolldaiga Hills Research Programme","Makerere University","Obafemi Awolowo University","Université Paul Sabatier Toulouse III"],"keyword":["Disease resistance","Introgression","Population structure"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"warthog genomes resolve an evolutionary conundrum and reveal introgression of disease resistance genes","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msac134","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-431959","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9250280","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85133836781","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35779009","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-4066f31b-f9e9-43e2-95a3-7bbcc4833bcf"],"keyword":["African phylogeography","Phacochoerus evolution","disease resistance","introgression","population structure"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3910A692DE1C113B0D6AE8E6D16E940D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/315851985/msac134.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msac134","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Biology, Computational and RNA Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"u