":"1","first":"Inger Kathrine","key":"seierstadpau","last":"Seierstad","locid":"719f8491-e2c2-438a-9b6a-99a14512be74","name":"Seierstad, Inger Kathrine","orcid":"0000-0003-1294-2292","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bo Møllesøe","key":"vintherpau","last":"Vinther","locid":"0179bd8e-644a-4f98-a641-1bc374aabdd8","name":"Vinther, Bo Møllesøe","orcid":"0000-0002-6078-771X","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Joseph S","key":"harrisonpau","last":"Harrison","name":"Harrison, Joseph S","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Elliott","key":"streetpau","last":"Street","name":"Street, Elliott","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jørgen Peder","key":"steffensenpau","last":"Steffensen","locid":"688738da-b41f-4ff3-bb45-9870e6db5c7e","name":"Steffensen, Jørgen Peder","orcid":"0000-0002-5516-1093","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"F.","key":"wilhelmspau","last":"Wilhelms","name":"Wilhelms, F.","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Siwan M.","key":"daviespau","last":"Davies","name":"Davies, Siwan M.","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.quascirev.2022.107596","issn":["02773791","1873457X"],"issn_org":["02773791"],"journal":"Quaternary Science Reviews","paperid":"107596","status":"p","title":"Quaternary Science Reviews","volume":"292"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85136493187"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4a564dc5-fe6f-4f5d-a2cd-861abce9a13c"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Volcanism and the Greenland ice cores : A new tephrochronological framework for the last glacial-interglacial transition (LGIT) based on cryptotephra deposits in three ice cores","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-437830","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In the Arctic tundra, snowmelt is followed by soil thaw allowing water and dissolved nutrients to move downslope. However, the fate of the released nitrogen (N) remains unclear, which includes the fraction of N that is lost to downslope transport or converted to N gasses. We have quantified the release of NO3− into the soil solution and the loss of gaseous N upon thaw and up to a month after first thaw in an Arctic hillslope in W Greenland. We further investigated which factors of the slope ecosystem that influence the NO3− concentrations and N2O fluxes throughout two snowmelt and growing seasons using a Structural Equation Model (SEM) linking physical, biological and biogeochemical characteristics across the slope. Snowmelt controls growing season onset, but varies in the landscape. To account for this, we normalized the spatiotemporal variation in snowmelt and soil thaw by measuring NO3− release and N2O loss in a controlled laboratory thaw experiment with topsoil cores from along the slope. We furthermore normalized seasonal progression of ecosystem variables in space based on the first day of soil thaw in the field. We tested the variable Day After Soil Thaw (DAST) as the temporal driver in our SEM, and found that season progression is the most important factor to describe patterns in NO3− concentrations and N2O fluxes. We conclude that DAST is a useful tool for analysing seasonal patterns in a spatially heterogeneous snowmelt landscape and between different snowmelt years. When normalizing based on first day of soil thaw, we saw that the decreasing NO3− content over the season did not control the increasing N2O emissions. Rather, nitrification replaced denitrification as the main N2O -source during the growing season, where soil temperatures increased and soil moisture decreased. The gaseous N loss from the slope during the first month of thaw was minor and amounted to 1% of the annual N deposition. A NO3− pulse released into solution after 24 h of thaw, when meltwater moves along the slope and connects upslope with downslope ecosystems, thus constituted a “hot moment” for interaction between landscape N pools, but the NO3− was immobilized by microorganisms or taken up by plants rather than denitrified and did thus not constitute a hot moment for N2O emissions. Thus, our results regarding what drives the fate of spring-thaw released N in the sloping Arctic landscape highlight the importance of snowmelt timing and the following number of Day After Soil Thaw as a normalizing factor for biogeochemical processes. This provides an analytical concept for reducing spatial and inter-annual variability to understand general seasonal patterns otherwise hidden.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Laura Helene","Mortensen, Louise Hindborg","Ambus, Per Lennart","Michelsen, Anders","Elberling, Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-451762"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Gothenburg"],"keyword":["Arctic tundra","NO emission","Nitrogen cycling","Snowmelt","Structural equation model","Topography"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"normalizing time in terms of space what drives the fate of spring thaw released nitrogen in a sloping arctic landscape","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/358630759/1_s2.0_S0038071722002978_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-451762","identifier":[{"id":"85139416903","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-61788210-92fc-48db-b58f-2f0ac34a7198"],"keyword":["Arctic tundra","NO emission","Nitrogen cycling","Snowmelt","Structural equation model","Topography"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FC0B2F479862C3A2CFF762396020DFC6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/358630759/1_s2.0_S0038071722002978_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"university of gothenburg","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gothenburg","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Geosciences and Natural Resource Management, Geography","University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Laura Helene","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"1d17b04f-b957-4a10-977e-3e525b9e3a2d","name":"Rasmussen, Laura Helene","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Louise H.","key":"mortensenpau","last":"Mortensen","locid":"9514cd40-82b9-4d13-88a2-ac7dcdb37865","name":"Mortensen, Louise Hindborg","name_org":"Mortensen, Louise H.","orcid":"0000-0002-5415-3251","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Per","key":"ambuspau","last":"Ambus","locid":"e3fc10b5-9fcd-494b-a3bc-dcfbede40f90","name":"Ambus, Per Lennart","name_org":"Ambus, Per","orcid":"0000-0001-7580-524X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anders","key":"michelsenpau","last":"Michelsen","locid":"5f62798d-50c6-41c3-8018-18babf5e5046","name":"Michelsen, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-9541-8658","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Bo","key":"elberlingpau","last":"Elberling","locid":"e12e4d80-dea0-424d-82bf-4dc019f65547","name":"Elberling, Bo","orcid":"0000-0002-6023-885X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.soilbio.2022.108840","issn":["00380717","18793428"],"issn_org":["00380717"],"journal":"Soil Biology & Biochemistry","paperid":"108840","status":"p","title":"Soil Biology & Biochemistry","title_org":"Soil Biology and Biochemistry","volume":"175"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85139416903"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-61788210-92fc-48db-b58f-2f0ac34a7198"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Normalizing time in terms of space : What drives the fate of spring thaw-released nitrogen in a sloping Arctic landscape?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-451762","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background: Quantitative whole-body PET/MRI relies on accurate patient-specific MRI-based attenuation correction (AC) of PET, which is a non-trivial challenge, especially for the anatomically complex head and neck region. We used a deep learning model developed for dose planning in radiation oncology to derive MRI-based attenuation maps of head and neck cancer patients and evaluated its performance on PET AC. Methods: Eleven head and neck cancer patients, referred for radiotherapy, underwent CT followed by PET/MRI with acquisition of Dixon MRI. Both scans were performed in radiotherapy position. PET AC was performed with three different patient-specific attenuation maps derived from: (1) Dixon MRI using a deep learning network (PETDeep). (2) Dixon MRI using the vendor-provided atlas-based method (PETAtlas). (3) CT, serving as reference (PETCT). We analyzed the effect of the MRI-based AC methods on PET quantification by assessing the average voxelwise error within the entire body, and the error as a function of distance to bone/air. The error in mean uptake within anatomical regions of interest and the tumor was also assessed. Results: The average (± standard deviation) PET voxel error was 0.0 ± 11.4% for PETDeep and −1.3 ± 21.8% for PETAtlas. The error in mean PET uptake in bone/air was much lower for PETDeep (−4%/12%) than for PETAtlas (−15%/84%) and PETDeep also demonstrated a more rapidly decreasing error with distance to bone/air affecting only the immediate surroundings (less than 1 cm). The regions with the largest error in mean uptake were those containing bone (mandible) and air (larynx) for both methods, and the error in tumor mean uptake was −0.6 ± 2.0% for PETDeep and −3.5 ± 4.6% for PETAtlas. Conclusion: The deep learning network for deriving MRI-based attenuation maps of head and neck cancer patients demonstrated accurate AC and exceeded the performance of the vendor-provided atlas-based method both overall, on a lesion-level, and in vicinity of challenging regions such as bone and air.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Olin, Anders B.","Hansen, Adam Espe","Rasmussen, Jacob H.","Jakoby, Björn","Berthelsen, Anne K.","Ladefoged, Claes N.","Kjaer, Andreas","Fischer, Barbara Malene","Andersen, Flemming Littrup"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-481044"],"filters":{"added":"2022-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","King's College London","Siemens","University of Surrey"],"keyword":["Deep learning","Head and neck cancer","MR AC","PET MRI"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"deep learning for dixon mri based attenuation correction in pet mri of head and neck cancer patients","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40658-022-00449-z","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-481044","identifier":[{"id":"85126292853","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35294629","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8927520","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-92a35ced-ad25-492a-8d02-2d3d42708104"],"keyword":["Deep learning","Head and neck cancer","MR-AC","PET/MRI"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E1FC7887EBEABACA72C06806B41DC9DF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/313867400/Deep.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s40658-022-00449-z","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Cluster for Molecular Imaging"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"siemens","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Siemens","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of surrey","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Surrey","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"king s college london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"King's College London","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Anders B.","key":"olinpau","last":"Olin","locid":"d81d9238-eddc-4496-b609-4e95fafdd9c3","name":"Olin, Anders B.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Adam E.","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"b36de088-99f8-4b75-bef2-96150f50a6e7","name":"Hansen, Adam Espe","name_org":"Hansen, Adam E.","orcid":"0000-0002-6457-1537","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Jacob H.","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Jacob H.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","first":"Björn","key":"jakobypau","last":"Jakoby","name":"Jakoby, Björn","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anne K.","key":"berthelsenpau","last":"Berthelsen","name":"Berthelsen, Anne K.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Claes N.","key":"ladefogedpau","last":"Ladefoged","name":"Ladefoged, Claes N.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Andreas","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"23e31554-6330-4153-9443-43d6e2951b3d","name":"Kjaer, Andreas","name_org":"Kjær, Andreas","orcid":"0000-0002-2706-5547","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1 5","first":"Barbara M.","key":"fischerpau","last":"Fischer","locid":"a87f805a-ba44-45e2-b257-59a6f2976711","name":"Fischer, Barbara Malene","name_org":"Fischer, Barbara M.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Flemming L.","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"abbdc93c-3b5b-4a18-a96f-3be1e9e7da09","name":"Andersen, Flemming Littrup","name_org":"Andersen, Flemming L.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35294629"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s40658-022-00449-z","issn":["21977364"],"issue":"1","journal":"EJNMMI Physics","paperid":"20","status":"p","title":"EJNMMI Physics","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85126292853"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-92a35ced-ad25-492a-8d02-2d3d42708104"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Deep learning for Dixon MRI-based attenuation correction in PET/MRI of head and neck cancer patients","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-481044","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: The accuracy of hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) as a glycemic marker in patients with type 2 diabetes (T2D) receiving hemodialysis (HD) remains unknown. To assess accuracy, we compared HbA1c and fructosamine levels with interstitial glucose measured by continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) in patients with T2D receiving HD. METHODS: Thirty patients in the HD group and 36 patients in the control group (T2D and an estimated glomerular filtration rate >60 mL/min/1.73 m2) completed the study period of 17 weeks. CGM (Ipro2®, Medtronic) was performed 5 times for periods of up to 7 days (with 4-week intervals) during a 16-week period. HbA1c (mmol/mol), the estimated mean plasma glucose from HbA1c (eMPGA1c [mmol/L]) and fructosamine (μmol/L) was measured at week 17 and compared with mean sensor glucose levels from CGM. FINDINGS: In the HD group, mean sensor glucose was 1.4 mmol/L (95% confidence interval [CI]: 1.0-1.8) higher than the eMPGA1c, whereas the difference for controls was 0.1 mmol/L (95% CI: -0.1-[0.4]; p < 0.001). Adjusted for mean sensor glucose, HbA1c was lower in the HD group (-7.3 mmol/mol, 95% CI: -10.0-[-4.7]) than in the control group (p < 0.001), with no difference detected for fructosamine (p = 0.64). DISCUSSION: HbA1c evaluated by CGM underestimates plasma glucose levels in patients receiving HD. The underestimation represents a clinical challenge in optimizing glycemic control in the HD population. Fructosamine is unaffected by the factors affecting HbA1c and appears to be more accurate for glycemic monitoring. CGM or fructosamine could thus complement HbA1c in obtaining more accurate glycemic control in this patient group.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Bomholt, Tobias","Rix, Marianne","Almdal, Thomas","Knop, Filip K.","Rosthøj, Susanne","Heinrich, Niels Søndergaard","Jørgensen, Morten B","Larsson, Anders","Hilsted, Linda","Feldt-Rasmussen, Bo","Hornum, Mads"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-529261"],"filters":{"added":"2022-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Uppsala University"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the accuracy of hemoglobin a000000000001c and fructosamine evaluated by long term continuous glucose monitoring in patients with type 000000000002 diabetes undergoing hemodialysis","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-529261","identifier":[{"id":"85116828722","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34583354","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-e3791250-d217-4189-90e9-c8746450cdb8"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0477A3106E470C5890281F1C83620AAC"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"M