{"abstract":"Context: Data on long-term safety of growth hormone (GH) replacement in adults with GH deficiency (GHD) are needed. Objective: We aimed to evaluate the safety of GH in the full KIMS (Pfizer International Metabolic Database) cohort. Methods: The worldwide, observational KIMS study included adults and adolescents with confirmed GHD. Patients were treated with GH (Genotropin [somatropin]; Pfizer, NY) and followed through routine clinical practice. Adverse events (AEs) and clinical characteristics (eg, lipid profile, glucose) were collected. Results: A cohort of 15 809 GH-treated patients were analyzed (mean follow-up of 5.3 years). AEs were reported in 51.2% of patients (treatment-related in 18.8%). Crude AE rate was higher in patients who were older, had GHD due to pituitary/hypothalamic tumors, or adult-onset GHD. AE rate analysis adjusted for age, gender, etiology, and follow-up time showed no correlation with GH dose. A total of 606 deaths (3.8%) were reported (146 by neoplasms, 71 by cardiac/vascular disorders, 48 by cerebrovascular disorders). Overall, de novo cancer incidence was comparable to that in the general population (standard incidence ratio 0.92; 95% CI, 0.83-1.01). De novo cancer risk was significantly lower in patients with idiopathic/congenital GHD (0.64; 0.43-0.91), but similar in those with pituitary/hypothalamic tumors or other etiologies versus the general population. Neither adult-onset nor childhood-onset GHD was associated with increased de novo cancer risks. Neutral effects were observed in lipids/fasting blood glucose levels. Conclusion: These final KIMS cohort data support the safety of long-term GH replacement in adults with GHD as prescribed in routine clinical practice.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Johannsson, Gudmundur","Touraine, Philippe","Feldt-Rasmussen, Ulla","Pico, Antonio","Vila, Greisa","Mattsson, Anders F.","Carlsson, Martin","Korbonits, Márta","Van Beek, Andr Crossed D.sign© P.","Wajnrajch, Michael P.","Gomez, Roy","Yuen, Kevin C.J."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-395306"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Austria","France","Netherlands","Spain","Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Assistance publique – Hôpitaux de Paris","Hospital General Universitario de Alicante","Instituto de Salud Carlos III","Medical University of Vienna","Miguel Hernández University","New York University","Pfizer","Queen Mary University of London","Sahlgrenska University Hospital","University of Arizona","University of Groningen"],"keyword":["Cancer","Growth hormone","Hypopituitarism","Safety","adult growth hormone deficiency"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"long term safety of growth hormone in adults with growth hormone deficiency overview of 000000000015 000000000809 gh treated patients","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1210/clinem/dgac199","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-395306","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9202689","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85132454167","type":"scopus"},{"id":"35368070","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-0316ab54-b1c4-4346-9d9e-8505a2b02bc9"],"keyword":["KIMS","adult growth hormone deficiency","cancer","growth hormone","hypopituitarism","safety"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F1BA5345A9B374D9988E86017B3966C0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/328428096/Long_term_Safety_of_Growth_Hormone_in_Adults.pdf","ver