ed plasmas","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-352409","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Zinc is essential for all organisms and yet detrimental at elevated levels. Hence, homeostasis of this metal is tightly regulated. The Zrt/Irt-like proteins (ZIPs) represent the only zinc importers in metazoans. Mutations in human ZIPs cause serious disorders, but the mechanism by which ZIPs transfer zinc remains elusive. Hitherto, structural information is only available for a model member, BbZIP, and as a single, ion-bound conformation, precluding mechanistic insights. Here, we elucidate an inward-open metal-free BbZIP structure, differing substantially in the relative positions of the two separate domains of ZIPs. With accompanying coevolutional analyses, mutagenesis, and uptake assays, the data point to an elevator-type transport mechanism, likely shared within the ZIP family, unifying earlier functional data. Moreover, the structure reveals a previously unknown ninth transmembrane segment that is important for activity in vivo. Our findings outline the mechanistic principles governing ZIP-protein transport and enhance the molecular understanding of ZIP-related disorders.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Wiuf, Anders","Steffen, Jonas Hyld","Becares, Eva Ramos","Grønberg, Christina","Mahato, Dhani Ram","Rasmussen, Søren Gøgsig Faarup","Andersson, Magnus","Croll, Tristan","Gotfryd, Kamil","Gourdon, Pontus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-445057"],"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Umeå University","University of Cambridge"],"keyword":["Environment","Family","Identification","Insights","MODEL"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the two domain elevator type mechanism of zinc transporting zip proteins","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-445057","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9278863","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"35857505","type":"pmid"},{"id":"000826385700020","type":"wos"},{"id":"85134645174","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-56695617-d478-48dc-b763-b774bf6a6842"],"keyword":["ENVIRONMENT","FAMILY","IDENTIFICATION","INSIGHTS","MODEL","SYMPORTER"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6AB4C958FD5B3420107D09D26BCA4CC6"],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Molecular Cardiology and Membrane Proteins"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"umeå university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Umeå University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"university of cambridge","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Cambridge","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Anders","key":"wiufpau","last":"Wiuf","locid":"b98badcd-0e9c-4dc9-a8a2-ad06564499c6","name":"Wiuf, Anders","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jonas Hyld","key":"steffenpau","last":"Steffen","locid":"7e305ae1-3a9e-4053-b8da-f1f7f823e261","name":"Steffen, Jonas Hyld","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Eva Ramos","key":"becarespau","last":"Becares","locid":"8de16ba1-9be9-4696-906b-46ab22a3f7ab","name":"Becares, Eva Ramos","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Christina","key":"gronbergpau","last":"Gronberg","locid":"56c5a93f-3896-45f6-9856-ebd90bf4cb65","name":"Grønberg, Christina","name_org":"Gronberg, Christina","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Dhani Ram","key":"mahatopau","last":"Mahato","name":"Mahato, Dhani Ram","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Soren G. F.","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"916c4655-dd61-4f65-a56a-d19ed9efb5f4","name":"Rasmussen, Søren Gøgsig Faarup","name_org":"Rasmussen, Soren G. F.","orcid":"0000-0002-1853-1841","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Magnus","key":"anderssonpau","last":"Andersson","name":"Andersson, Magnus","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Tristan","key":"crollpau","last":"Croll","name":"Croll, Tristan","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kamil","key":"gotfrydpau","last":"Gotfryd","locid":"5dbce8e5-22d9-4a8c-b7c6-1ee639bbc938","name":"Gotfryd, Kamil","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Pontus","key":"gourdonpau","last":"Gourdon","locid":"c0015a81-261a-4e28-9fd2-c69fb1fca393","name":"Gourdon, Pontus","orcid":"0000-0002-8631-3539","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35857505"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1126/sciadv.abn4331","issn":["23752548"],"issue":"28","journal":"Science Advances","paperid":"4331","status":"p","title":"Science Advances","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85134645174"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-56695617-d478-48dc-b763-b774bf6a6842"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The two-domain elevator-type mechanism of zinc-transporting ZIP proteins","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-445057","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"STUDY QUESTION: Do paracetamol (N-acetyl-para-aminophenol (APAP) or acetaminophen) and/or its metabolites affect human sperm Ca2+-signalling and function? SUMMARY ANSWER: While APAP itself does not interact with Ca2+-signalling in human sperm, its metabolite N-arachidonoyl phenolamine (AM404), produced via fatty acid amide hydrolase (FAAH), interferes with human sperm Ca2+-signalling and function through a suggested CatSper channel-dependent action. WHAT IS KNOWN ALREADY: Studies have shown that adult men with high urinary levels of over-the-counter mild analgesic APAP have impaired sperm motility and increased time-to-pregnancy. STUDY DESIGN, SIZE, DURATION: This study consists of (i) an in vivo human pharmaceutical APAP exposure experiment to understand to what degree APAP reaches the sperm cells in the seminal fluid; (ii) in vitro calcium imaging and functional experiments in freshly donated human sperm cells to investigate CatSper channel-dependent activation by APAP and its metabolites; and (iii) experiments to understand the in situ capabilities of human sperm cells to form APAP metabolite AM404. PARTICIPANTS/MATERIALS, SETTING, METHODS: Three healthy young males participated in the in vivo human exposure experiment after prior consent. Human semen samples were provided by healthy young volunteer donors after prior consent on the day of the in vitro experiments. MAIN RESULTS AND THE ROLE OF CHANCE: Pharmaceutical APAP exposure reaches the seminal plasma in high micromolar concentrations and accumulates in the seminal plasma between 3 and 5 days of exposure (P-value 0.023). APAP and its primary metabolite 4-aminophenol (4AP) do not interact with human sperm Ca2+-signalling. Instead, the APAP metabolite AM404 produced via FAAH interferes with human sperm Ca2+-signalling through a CatSper-dependent action. Also, AM404 significantly increases sperm cell penetration into viscous mucous (P-value of 0.003). FAAH is functionally expressed in human sperm cells in the neck/midpiece region, as evidenced by immunohistochemical staining and the ability of human sperm cells to hydrolyse the fluorogenic FAAH substrate arachidonyl 7-amino, 4-methyl coumarin amide in an FAAH-dependent manner. Importantly, human sperm cells have the capacity to form AM404 in situ after exposure to 4AP (P-value 0.0402 compared to vehicle-treated sperm cells). LIMITATIONS, REASONS FOR CAUTION: The experiments were conducted largely in vitro. Future studies are needed to test whether APAP can disrupt human sperm function in vivo through the action of AM404. WIDER IMPLICATIONS OF THE FINDINGS: We hypothesize that these observations could, at least in part, be responsible for the negative association between male urinary APAP concentrations, sperm motility and time-to-pregnancy. STUDY FUNDING/COMPETING INTEREST(S): D.M.K. is funded by the Lundbeck Foundation, grant number R324-2019-1881, and the Svend Andersen Foundation. A.R. is funded by a BRIDGE-Translational Excellence Programme grant funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation, grant agreement number: NNF18SA0034956. All authors declare no competing interests. TRIAL REGISTRATION NUMBER: N/A.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Rehfeld, A","Frederiksen, H.","Rasmussen, R. H.","David, A.","Chaker, J.","Nielsen, B. S.","Nielsen, J. E.","Juul, Anders","Skakkebæk, Niels Erik","Kristensen, David Møbjerg"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-452950"],"filters":{"added":"2022-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Rennes"],"keyword":["Adult","Arachidonic Acids","Calcium Channels metabolism","Calcium metabolism","Humans","Male","Progesterone metabolism","Sperm Motility","Spermatozoa metabolism"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"human sperm cells can form paracetamol metabolite am000000000404 that directly interferes with sperm calcium signalling and function through a catsper dependent mechanism","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-452950","identifier":[{"id":"35259261","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85134636815","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-637cda20-2473-4a40-9dcc-841041beb812"],"keyword":["Acetaminophen/pharmacology","Adult","Arachidonic Acids","Calcium Channels/metabolism","Calcium/metabolism","Humans","Male","Pharmaceutical Preparations/metabolism","Progesterone/metabolism","Sperm Motility","Spermatozoa/metabolism"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6CAE811C5B5E2D0C40A15648E831E71F"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"university of rennes","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Rennes","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Paediatrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"A","key":"rehfeldapau","last":"Rehfeld","name":"Rehfeld, A","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"H.","key":"frederiksenhpau","last":"Frederiksen","name":"Frederiksen, H.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"R. H.","key":"rasmussenrhpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, R. H.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"A.","key":"davidapau","last":"David","name":"David, A.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"J.","key":"chakerjpau","last":"Chaker","name":"Chaker, J.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"B. S.","key":"nielsenbspau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, B. S.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"J. E.","key":"nielsenjepau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, J. E.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"A","key":"juulanderspau","last":"Juul","locid":"f9b11983-c7c8-4111-b119-4de471572898","name":"Juul, Anders","name_org":"Juul, A","orcid":"0000-0002-0534-4350","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"N. E.","key":"skakkebæknielserikpau","last":"Skakkebæk","locid":"79cf30ee-3d35-4c17-bd83-b233c78d01b9","name":"Skakkebæk, Niels Erik","name_org":"Skakkebæk, N. E.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","first":"D. M.","key":"kristensendavidmøbjergpau","last":"Kristensen","locid":"f6941eac-bffb-43dd-86a7-6a2ce08f1303","name":"Kristensen, David Møbjerg","name_org":"Kristensen, D. M.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35259261"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/humrep/deac042","issn":["02681161","14602350"],"issn_org":["02681161"],"issue":"5","journal":"Human Reproduction","pages":"922-935","status":"p","title":"Human Reproduction","title_org":"Human reproduction (Oxford, England)","volume":"37"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85134636815"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-637cda20-2473-4a40-9dcc-841041beb812"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Human sperm cells can form paracetamol metabolite AM404 that directly interferes with sperm calcium signalling and function through a CatSper-dependent mechanism","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-452950","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In this study, 28 \"historical\" clinical freeze-dried nontuberculous mycobacterial isolates collected from 1948 to 1957, were analyzed by investigating their viability and performing whole genome sequencing (WGS) on DNA extracted (i) directly from freeze-dried cells versus (ii) after culturing, to determine cell properties and DNA quality after centuries of freeze-dried storage. The isolated DNA was sequenced on the Illumina MiSeq platform and data quality evaluated analyzing the per-base quality scores of paired-end sequencing reads as well as the overall contiguity of resulting de novo assemblies. After 72 years in storage, all freeze-dried isolates were viable, and showed no signs of cell damage and limited signs of contamination when reculturing. They were recultured without problems and identified through WGS with only four of 13 parameters showing statistical significance based on sequence data obtained directly from the freeze-dried cells versus after reculturing, indicating no DNA degradation. Thus, mycobacteria can be whole genome sequenced successfully directly from freeze-dried material without prior recultivation, saving laboratory time and resources, and emphasizing the value of freeze-drying for long-term storage. Our study lays the groundwork for further genomic investigations of freeze-dried bacterial isolates, and the approximately 4,000 historical isolates in our collection will provide a unique opportunity to investigate mycobacterial DNA from a variety of NTM species unexposed to antimicrobials, some maybe still undescribed species. IMPORTANCE The genus Mycobacterium was described more than a century ago and new species are continuously identified and described. There is an ongoing discussion about an increase in the incidence of disease caused by nontuberculous mycobacteria (NTM). How the different bacteria looked before exposure to antibiotics can only be investigated by looking at strains from before the antibiotic era. Strains from that era will be stored in different ways, for example by freeze-drying. The question is how to investigate these strains, and if they are still viable, whether they need to be cultured, and if that changes the DNA. Here, we test all these parameters on freeze-dried strains and show that NGS can be applied directly without culturing.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Iversen, Xenia Emilie Sinding","Norman, Anders","Folkvardsen, Dorte Bek","Svensson, Erik","Rasmussen, Erik Michael","Lillebaek, Troels"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-469606"],"filters":{"added":"2022-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"OT":["International Reference Laboratory of Mycobacteriology"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"successful direct whole genome sequencing and revivification of freeze dried nontuberculous mycobacteria after more than half a century of storage","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.00310-22","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-469606","identifier":[{"id":"PMC9241853","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"35587637","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85133214001","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-7f3851bf-8c21-4acb-b887-5f2629eedc86"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C2C521673AD507C33C229A5E77D1BB6F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/312472793/Successful.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.00310-22","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["International Reference Laboratory of Mycobacteriology"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ssi statens serum institut","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-SSI","l3nn":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","namevar":["Statens Serum Institut"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-SSI","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Global Health Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Xenia Emilie Sinding","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","name":"Iversen, Xenia Emilie Sinding","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Anders","key":"normanpau","last":"Norman","name":"Norman, Anders","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Dorte Bek","key":"folkvardsenpau","last":"Folkvardsen","name":"Folkvardsen, Dorte Bek","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Erik","key":"svenssonpau","last":"Svensson","name":"Svensson, Erik","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Erik Michael","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Erik Michael","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Troels","key":"lillebækpau","last":"Lillebæk","locid":"4c4e219b-6ac0-4067-a078-ef52b9c2beff","name":"Lillebaek, Troels","name_org":"Lillebæk, Troels","orcid":"0000-0002-2601-2718","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["35587637"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1128/spectrum.00310-22","issn":["21650497"],"issue":"3","journal":"Microbiology Spectrum","paperid":"e0031022","status":"p","title":"Microbiology Spectrum","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85133214001"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-7f3851bf-8c21-4acb-b887-5f2629eedc86"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Successful Direct Whole Genome Sequencing and Revivification of Freeze-Dried Nontuberculous Mycobacteria after More than Half a Century of Storage","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-469606","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Imaging-guided percutaneous biopsy of renal masses is regarded as safe and is widely used for histopathology diagnosis before treatment. Recommended observation time after tumour biopsy varies in international guidelines and the literature is sparse. PURPOSE: To assess the effect of a 1-h post-biopsy observation time of percutaneous image-guided renal mass biopsy. MATERIAL AND METHODS: This was a single-centre retrospective study. During January 2015 to September 2019, a total of 484 patients underwent renal mass biopsies. 4-h-observation-group: 178 patients and 1-h-observation-group 306 patients. All records were retrospectively reviewed, and data such as complications was obtained and compared between the two groups. RESULTS: A total complication rate of 4.5% ( n = 22) without any major complications (Cardiovascular and Interventional Radiological Society of Europe-grade (CIRSE) 5-6). Furthermore, a non-significant difference of 1.3% of the 1-h group and 3.4% in the 4-h group experiencing complications was found ( p = .18). A total biopsy-accuracy of 84% was observed. CONCLUSION: This study shows that renal mass biopsy is safe with no major complications. This suggests that an outpatient approach with 1-h-observation time can be safely implemented for renal mass biopsy.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Valtersson, John","Rasmussen, Benjamin Schnack","Elgborn, Anders","Lund, Lars","Graumann, Ole"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-579807"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Graumann, Ole"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"one hour observation of patients after image guided percutaneous renal mass biopsy","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all",