{"abstract":"Background Pulmonary rehabilitation (PR) is a cornerstone in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) management. However, PR adherence is generally low, and barriers include availability, economic issues, motivation and an inability to attend or perform physical training. Therefore, alternative, evidence- based PR activities are required. Singing may have benefits for quality of life (QoL), respiratory control and well-being in COPD, but the impact on the PR key outcome, physical exercise capacity, is uncertain. Methods In this randomised controlled trial (NCT03280355), we investigated the effectiveness of 10 weeks of PR, including either “Singing for Lung Health” (SLH) training or standard physical exercise training (PExT). The primary outcome was a change in exercise capacity (6-min walk distance (6MWD)) from baseline to post-PR. Secondary outcomes were changes in QoL (St George’s Respiratory Questionnaire (SGRQ)), Hospital Anxiety and Depression Score (HADS), lung function, dyspnoea and adherence. Results We included 270 COPD patients, and 195 completed the study. Demographics across groups were comparable, and both groups improved significantly in 6MWD and SGRQ score. SLH was non-inferior to PExT in improving 6MWD (mean±SD 13.1±36.3 m versus 14.1±32.3 m, p=0.81; difference 1.0 m, 95% CI −7.3–9.3m) with 21.8% and 25.0%, respectively (p=0.57), reaching the 6MWD minimal important difference of 30 m. We found no significant between-group differences concerning SGRQ, HADS, lung function, dyspnoea or adherence. Conclusion Our study suggests that SLH is non-inferior to PExT in improving 6MWD during a 10-week PR programme. Future studies addressing reproducibility, long-term effects and health economics are needed.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Løkke, Anders","Kaasgaard, Mette","Andreasson, Karen Hjerrild","Rasmussen, Daniel Bech","Hilberg, Ole","Vuust, Peter","Bodtger, Uffe"],"cuid":["S02-096.51-023680"],"filters":{"added":"2022-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Exercise","Humans","Lung","Pulmonary Disease Chronic Obstructive","Singing","Thorax"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDAM"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDAM","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reply the effectiveness of singing versus exercise training","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"djc","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85131271161","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-096.51-023680","identifier":[{"id":"34949703","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85131271161","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-9759bb20-1dc4-4c34-b6bf-37355af6d28d","sdu-8a6ccc13-58b0-4db9-92ff-200c4e67fd6d"],"keyword":["Exercise","Humans","Lung","Pulmonary Disease, Chronic Obstructive","Singing","Thorax"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C2596C0E33F97FCC4BD51221C08D29D9"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital, Research Unit of Medicine (Vejle)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Center for Music In the Brain"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Zealand University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"rdam the royal danish academy of music","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Artistic Higher Education Institutions","l3":"DI-ART-RDAM","l3nn":"RDAM The Royal Danish Academy of Music","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RDAM The Royal Danish Academy of Music","namevar":["Royal Academy of Music"],"nora_code":"DI-ART-RDAM","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Løkke","key":"ottesenpau","last":"Ottesen","locid":"db3d3870-0459-4783-8f0b-6a8e5d9dce78","name":"Løkke, Anders","name_org":"Ottesen, Anders Løkke","orcid":"0000-0002-6905-4141","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3 4 1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mette","key":"kaasgaardpau","last":"Kaasgaard","locid":"fc5784b6-66d8-4b92-8c4a-74bf4628c9c3","name":"Kaasgaard, Mette","orcid":"0000-0003-3833-2943","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Karen Hjerrild","key":"andreassonpau","last":"Andreasson","locid":"8af88feb-2896-4499-89bd-750df2e82461","name":"Andreasson, Karen Hjerrild","orcid":"0000-0002-8752-9614","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel Bech","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"18525822-35fd-4cd8-afdd-a608be44cc84","name":"Rasmussen, Daniel Bech","orcid":"0000-0003-1213-9483","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter","key":"vuustpau","last":"Vuust","locid":"2c697ab6-9b06-4a16-a687-c8cdbd56f661","name":"Vuust, Peter","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ole","key":"hilbergpau","last":"Hilberg","locid":"6c545c65-1d94-45a3-a8b4-f90eae06d248","name":"Hilberg, Ole","orcid":"0000-0002-3075-3463","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Uffe","key":"bodtgerpau","last":"Bodtger","locid":"2f4e70da-63bb-4b8d-af09-2a959d6df5e8","name":"Bodtger, Uffe","orcid":"0000-0002-1231-9209","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34949703"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1183/13993003.03051-2021","issn":["09031936","13993003"],"issn_org":["09031936"],"issue":"6","journal":"European Respiratory Journal","paperid":"2103051","publisher":"European Respiratory Society","status":"p","title":"European Respiratory Journal","volume":"59"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85131271161"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Artistic Higher Education Institutions","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Artistic Higher Education Institutions","DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-ART-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-9759bb20-1dc4-4c34-b6bf-37355af6d28d","sdu-8a6ccc13-58b0-4db9-92ff-200c4e67fd6d"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Reply : The effectiveness of singing versus exercise training","type":"Journal Comment","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.51-023680","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Background: Total knee arthroplasty (TKA) is associated with moderate-to-severe postoperative pain despite multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia. Pain catastrophising or preoperative opioid therapy is associated with increased postoperative pain. Preoperative glucocorticoid improves pain after TKA, but dose-finding studies and benefit in high pain responders are lacking. Methods: A randomised double-blind controlled trial with preoperative high-dose intravenous dexamethasone 1 mg kg−1 or intermediate-dose dexamethasone 0.3 mg kg−1 in 88 patients undergoing TKA with preoperative pain catastrophising score >20 or regular opioid use was designed. The primary outcome was the proportion of patients experiencing moderate-to-severe pain (VAS >30) during a 5 m walk 24 h postoperatively. Secondary outcomes included pain at rest during nights and at passive leg raise, C-reactive protein, opioid use, quality of sleep, Quality of Recovery-15 and Opioid-Related Symptom Distress Scale, readmission, and complications. Results: Moderate-to-severe pain when walking 24 h postoperatively was reduced (high dose vs intermediate dose, 49% vs 79%; P<0.01), along with pain at leg raise at 24 and 48 h (14% vs 29%, P=0.02 and 12% vs 31%, P=0.03, respectively). C-reactive protein was reduced in the high-dose group at both 24 and 48 h (both P<0.01). Quality of Recovery-15 was also improved (P<0.01). Conclusions: When compared with preoperative dexamethasone 0.3 mg kg−1 i.v., dexamethasone 1 mg kg−1 reduced moderate-to-severe pain 24 h after TKA and improved recovery in high pain responders without apparent side-effects. Clinical trial registration: NCT03763734.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Nielsen, Niklas I.","Kehlet, Henrik","Gromov, Kirill","Troelsen, Anders","Husted, Henrik","Varnum, Claus","Kjærsgaard-Andersen, Per","Rasmussen, Lasse E.","Pleckaitiene, Lina","Foss, Nicolai Bang"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-043382"],"filters":{"added":"2022-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Niklas I."],"keyword":["Anaesthesia","Dexamethasone","Fast track surgery","Multimodal analgesia","Postoperative pain","Total knee arthroplasty","high dose steroids","high pain responders"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"high dose steroids in high pain responders undergoing total knee arthroplasty a randomised double blind trial","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2021.10.001","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Candys Foundation (unconditional grant) to HK."],"id":"S02-100.00-043382","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8787770","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34749994","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85118930006","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-70981fdb-0e5d-4ab6-9b47-f0b4cf6e3b58","sdu-58c96eff-89c5-44b6-aa6f-e9cf1250af42"],"keyword":["anaesthesia","dexamethasone","fast-track surgery","high pain responders","high-dose steroids","multimodal analgesia","postoperative pain","total knee arthroplasty"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-E75D118F9F3F3F10A54F4FA04D2C8F06"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/316692419/1_s2.0_S0007091221006255_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/200659471/Open_Access_Version.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2021.10.001","version":"Published Version"}],