{"abstract":"Interview/feature med Jon Dag Rasmussen.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Jacobsen, Johannes","Højberg Kamp, Anders","Rasmussen, Jon Dag"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-107101"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"pop","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-RUC"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-RUC"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"når et stamsted er et hjemsted interview feature","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-107101","ids":["aau-c4a7eb8e-1690-412a-a3ca-2163348873e1"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Popular","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-B7E22856F5BC67B0F0B29CBE1BFD8046"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ruc roskilde university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-RUC","l3nl":"Roskilde University","l3nn":"RUC Roskilde University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"RUC Roskilde University","namevar":["Roskilde University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-RUC","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of the Built Environment","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of the Built Environment, Division of Town, Housing and Property","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of the Built Environment, Universal Design Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Johannes","key":"jacobsenpau","last":"Jacobsen","name":"Jacobsen, Johannes","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"højbergkamppau","last":"Højberg Kamp","name":"Højberg Kamp, Anders","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jon Dag","key":"rasmussenped","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"ed14200c-4598-48f4-aaee-9dfe5347c5a5","name":"Rasmussen, Jon Dag","orcid":"0000-0002-5168-1761","rank":3,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"","first":"Anders Højberg","key":"kamppedh","last":"Kamp","name":"Kamp, Anders Højberg","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Johannes","key":"jacobsenpedh","last":"Jacobsen","name":"Jacobsen, Johannes","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788797098813"],"pages":"652-653","publisher":"Salvador Books","status":"p","subtitle":"Røverhistorier og scener fra byens beværtninger","title":"Stamsteder"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Roskilde University","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-c4a7eb8e-1690-412a-a3ca-2163348873e1"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Når et stamsted er et hjemsted (interview/feature)","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-107101","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"We have discovered an error pertinent to this paper, which occurred during the preparation of the marker dataset used in the four GWAS analyses described in our paper. We emphasize, however, that the overall result and conclusions of the GWAS analyses remain the same, and all the other analyses in the paper are unaffected by this error. In this corrigendum we specify which parts of the Results are affected and to what extent. The original text stated that the dataset previously used by Schork et al. (Schork et al., 2019) was used as the starting point for the SNP-based analyses in the paper. However, a different dataset, which should have gone through similar quality control (QC), was used. We have only recently discovered an error that occurred during the removal of some of the markers that had failed the preliminary QC measures. We summarize the preliminary and study-specific QC steps at the end of this corrigendum. The starting point for the analyses in this study was the raw dosage dataset, from which, due to an error, only some of the markers which had failed the preliminary QC were removed. As stated in the paper, however, during the GWAS procedure markers with INFO score < 0.8 and MAF < 0.01 in the respective GWAS samples were excluded from downstream analysis. Thus, the majority of markers which had failed the preliminary QC steps were removed by the above two filters, but not all of them. This applied only to the dataset used in the GWASs. The polygenic risk score (PRS) analyses used the QCed hard calls dataset, with the preliminary QC steps (as described below), and with individual genotypes with probability <0.9 removed. Therefore, the PRS results were not affected by the error. Similarly, the HLA dataset used in the study was based on a separate, independent imputation of HLA alleles, as described by Nudel et al. (Nudel et al., 2019a), and, therefore, the HLA analyses were not affected by this error. Lastly, the epidemiological (survival) analyses, which either did not use genetic data or used the PRS data, were not affected. When removing markers that failed the preliminary and study-specific QC steps (as summarized below), the overall results for all GWAS analyses (i.e. the results corresponding to Fig. 1 and Supplementary Fig. 3 in the original paper) are almost identical to the original ones. Of note, the strong signal in the HLA region on chromosome 6 for overall autoimmune disease (Fig. 1A) and the secondary signal for a comorbid diagnosis of an autoimmune disease and a mental disorder on chromosome 7 (Fig. 1B) are still observed. The top SNP in the GWAS for overall autoimmune disease is rs9275347 (chr6:32667623, effect allele A, other allele G, OR = 1.78, P = 6.397 × 10−34), which shows an effect similar to that of the previous top SNP. Additionally, this SNP is correlated with the previous top SNP (r2 = 0.45, p < 0.0001, χ2 = 89.2835, using LDlink with the CEU population). The top SNP on chromosome 7 in the secondary association peak with the comorbid diagnosis remains the same. Regarding the Index SNPs (Table 4), the top SNP in our original paper (rs1064173) was flagged only for missingness (>10%), and one other SNP (rs3957147) was flagged for missingness and for the HWE test. We note that the missingness filter was based on missingness post imputation, introduced during the removal of specific imputed genotypes which had a probability score <0.9 during the check. Thus, this missingness (24% in this case) is an outcome of the QC checks. Moreover, this step was not always employed in other large-scale studies in the literature, which typically filter imputed genotypes based on the INFO score (or equivalent) and MAF, for example the QC protocol for the UK Biobank data (https://doi.org/10.5523/bris.1ovaau5sxunp2cv8rcy88688v) or PGC studies (Schizophrenia Working Group of the Psychiatric Genomics Consortium, 2014). Hence, we believe that the association with this SNP may still be considered valid, but, nonetheless, we also report in this corrigendum the effect of removing it from the results. Regarding the bioinformatic functional analysis (Table 5, Fig. 2), as all gene associations were considered per each Index SNP separately, and the scores for each gene in Fig. 2 were independent of each other, the change here would be the removal of genes that were associated only with rs3957147, namely: PPT1, B2M, PRSS3, RACGAP1, and RNF5, from Table 5 and Fig. 2. If we then also remove genes associated only with rs1064173, this would include C4B, MICB, CYP21A1P, PDE4D, GRM4, IL21 and CD96. However, as stated above, we do not believe this latter change is absolutely necessary. We include as supplementary material to this corrigendum new plots for the GWASs, after removing markers which had failed the QC (preliminary QC + exclusion thresholds: INFO score < 0.8 and MAF < 0.01), and for the analysis in Fig. 2, after removing the above genes. We have also removed markers which had failed the QC from the summary statistics, whose markers now number 7264281 (Fig. 1A), 7264670 (Fig. 1B), 7264718 (Supplementary Fig. 3A), 7264387 (Supplementary Fig. 3B). We deeply apologize for the error and can provide both the new and the original summary statistics upon request. Summary of QC steps: in our previous papers (Nudel et al., 2020, 2019b), for the QCed dosage dataset, the following exclusion thresholds were employed for markers (post imputation), which constitute the preliminary QC: an INFO score (calculated with QCTOOL) < 0.2; a minor allele frequency (MAF) < 0.001; best-guess genotypes missing in >10% of subjects, where the missingness in imputed genotypes was determined by treating individual genotypes with probability < 0.9 as missing for the purpose of this check; Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium (HWE) P < 1 × 10−6 (in controls); a significant association with the genotyping wave. Although not previously mentioned, a small number of markers showed association with the imputation batch itself, and these were also removed, as were markers with differential missingness across psychiatric cases and controls (P < 1 × 10−6). In a more stringent QC, employed when only best-guess genotypes, or hard calls, were used, only genotypes with probability of 0.9 or above were retained, and markers were retained if they had an INFO score (following the imputation) of at least 0.8 and MAF of at least 0.01 (in the QCed sample). The study-specific QC in this paper employed the INFO score and MAF thresholds as per the stringent QC, but these were from the output of each respective GWAS.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Liu, Xueping","Nudel, Ron","Thompson, Wesley K.","Appadurai, Vivek","Schork, Andrew J.","Buil, Alfonso","Rasmussen, Simon","Allesøe, Rosa L.","Werge, Thomas","Mors, Ole","Børglum, Anders D","Hougaard, David M.","Mortensen, Preben Bo","Nordentoft, Merete","Benros, Michael E."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-195272"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Benros, Michael E."],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of California"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-LUND"],"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-NP-LUND","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"corrigendum to genetic factors underlying the bidirectional relationship between autoimmune and mental disorders findings from a danish population based study brain behav immun 000000000091 000000002021 000000000010 000000000023 brain behavior and immunity 000000002021 000000000091 000000000010 000000000023 s889159120300799 000000000010 000000001016 j bbi 000000002020 000000000006 000000000014","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"djc","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85110508941","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-195272","identifier":[{"id":"85110508941","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34052110","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-7387ab3a-5d8d-4dde-a2b7-40a9cc49b60b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DA9FCD19462A70DD0870785B3C5B1190"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"lundbeck foundation","l2":"DI-NP","l2n":"Non-Profit Organisations","l3":"DI-NP-LUND","l3nn":"Lundbeck Foundation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Lundbeck Foundation","namevar":["iPSYCH -The Lundbeck Foundation Initiative for Integrative Psychiatric Research"],"nora_code":"DI-NP-LUND","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Capitol Region of Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"university of california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Cli