{"abstract":"Childcare workers are reported to have poor cardiorespiratory fitness and health. The Goldilocks Work Principle argues that productive work should be designed with the right composition, intensity and alternations of physical behaviors so that workers get fit and healthy. The purpose of this study was to investigate: (1) composition, (2) intensity and (3) alternations of physical behaviors during work and leisure among childcare workers. Data were collected using accelerometers and heart rate monitors over five workdays among 51 childcare workers at five Danish childcare institutions. Workers mainly spent their work time sedentary (43.0%), spent little time (0.7%) at sufficiently high cardiometabolic intensity to increase cardiorespiratory fitness and often alternated between physical behaviors (67.0% occurred in bouts of <5 min). These findings indicate that the workers have a composition of behaviors at work dominated by sedentary time, little time with high cardiometabolic intensity, and frequent alternations between behaviors. During leisure, workers spent more time sedentary (59.4%), more time at high cardiometabolic intensity (3.4%) and less time occurred in bouts <5 min (38.7%). We see a potential for promoting cardiorespiratory fitness and health of childcare workers by redesigning the way they play with the children, so that work time with high cardiometabolic intensity is increased.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Schmidt, Kathrine Greby","Kildedal, Rasmus","Lerche, Anders Fritz","Vilhelmsen, Maja","Rasmussen, Charlotte Lund","Mathiassen, Svend Erik","Straker, Leon","Holtermann, Andreas"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-769463"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Kildedal, Rasmus","Schmidt, Kathrine Greby"],"country":["Australia","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Centre for Musculoskeletal Research, Department of Occupational and Public Health Sciences, University of Gävle, Gävle, Sweden.","Curtin University","School of Allied Health, Curtin University, Perth, Australia.","University of Gävle"],"keyword":["Childcare workers","Goldilocks Work Principle","Sundhedsmiljø","Workplace"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"does childcare work promote cardiorespiratory fitness and health a cross sectional study of danish childcare workers based on accelerometry and heart rate measurements","src":["nfa","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph182312496","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funding: This research was funded by the Danish Working Environment Research Fund (grant no. 18-2018-03)."],"id":"S02-100.00-769463","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8656773","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85119971780","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34886220","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["nfa-3adef314-ea50-4adb-b0d0-4d50311533b7","sdu-4e9d363c-6bda-49f7-ba86-622b640dace4"],"keyword":["Childcare workers","Goldilocks Work Principle","High cardiometabolic intensity","Physical behaviors","Sundhedsmiljø","Workplace"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-8C280622AB010885C90A6C9AC2D0B744"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph182312496","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/195289048/ijerph_18_12496.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"nfa national research centre for the working environment","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-WORK","l3nn":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","namevar":["The National Research Centre for the Working Environment","The National Research Centre for the Working Environment, Directors, Musculoskeletal Disorders and Physical Workload"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-WORK","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, Research Unit of Physical Activity and Health in Working life"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"se","key":"university of gävle","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gävle","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"centre for musculoskeletal research department of occupational and public health sciences university of gävle gävle sweden","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Centre for Musculoskeletal Research, Department of Occupational and Public Health Sciences, University of Gävle, Gävle, Sweden.","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"au","key":"curtin university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Curtin University","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"au","key":"school of allied health curtin university perth australia","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"School of Allied Health, Curtin University, Perth, Australia.","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kathrine Greby","key":"schmidtpau","last":"Schmidt","locid":"fe5a3b12-fb63-489c-845f-6c39c5e3d62f","name":"Schmidt, Kathrine Greby","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus","key":"kildedalpau","last":"Kildedal","locid":"4ef5cf07-a9e2-42bb-a947-f2c6e422f506","name":"Kildedal, Rasmus","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Fritz","key":"lerchepau","last":"Lerche","locid":"8ca85f92-8c87-439e-86fc-e2ca9cd5bbcb","name":"Lerche, Anders Fritz","orcid":"0000-0002-3199-6571","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Maja","key":"vilhelmsenpau","last":"Vilhelmsen","name":"Vilhelmsen, Maja","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte Lund","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Charlotte Lund","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Svend Erik","key":"mathiassenpau","last":"Mathiassen","name":"Mathiassen, Svend Erik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5 6","corresponding":null,"first":"Leon","key":"strakerpau","last":"Straker","name":"Straker, Leon","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andreas","key":"holtermannpau","last":"Holtermann","locid":"fd559bce-74b0-4973-a2d9-dd622af8b0c8","name":"Holtermann, Andreas","orcid":"0000-0003-4825-5697","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34886220"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/ijerph182312496","issn":["16604601","16617827"],"issn_org":["16617827"],"issue":"23","journal":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","paperid":"12496","publisher":"Multidisciplinary Digital Publishing Institute (MDPI)","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health","volume":"18"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85119971780"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["nfa-3adef314-ea50-4adb-b0d0-4d50311533b7","sdu-4e9d363c-6bda-49f7-ba86-622b640dace4"],"src":["NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Does childcare work promote cardiorespiratory fitness and health? A cross-sectional study of Danish childcare workers based on accelerometry and heart rate measurements","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-769463","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Although humans have impacted their environment over millennia, details of these impacts, especially on aquatic systems, is still surprisingly scarce despite potential disturbance by early land use. This study examined a high-resolution radiocarbon-dated Holocene record from the Danish Lake Væng, using geochemical and biological proxies, and related the observed impacts to other lake records with catchment disturbance. The results indicate a lengthy and varying history of aquatic eutrophication linked to human activity. Modest impacts on the lake coincided with the first signs of landscape disturbance during the Neolithic (c. 4500 cal. yrs BP). Observed impacts intensified in the Late Bronze and Pre-Roman Iron Age. Viking Age/Medieval deforestation and erosional inputs to the lake associated with new ploughing technology (1200 cal. yrs BP), however, led to a major reorganisation of the aquatic ecosystem. Filamentous bloom-forming cyanobacteria, common today in heavily culturally impacted lakes, reached a historical maxima. The lake ecosystem subsequently recovered somewhat but remains eutrophic to date. The erosion record from Lake Væng shows a striking similarity with other Danish lake records, especially the notable increase in Medieval Period catchment inputs, which are observed in other European lacustrine records. Numerous European lowland lakes may have shifted into a degraded ecological state millennia ago, but degradation intensified during the onset of the Medieval Period. Hence, assuming pre-industrial conditions as relatively pristine reference baselines for more recent cultural eutrophication could be flawed in landscapes intensively used by humans for millennia.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Bennike, Ole","Odgaard, Bent Vad","Moorhouse, Heather","McGowan, Suzanne","Siggaard-Andersen, Marie Louise","Turner, Benjamin","Schomacker, Anders","Jessen, Søren","Kazmierczak, Jolanta","Olsen, Jesper","Rasmussen, Peter","Kidmose, Jacob","Nisbeth, Catharina S.","Thorling, Lærke","Weckström, Kaarina"],"cuid":["S03-092.63-015229"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corpau":["Anders Schomacker","Benjamin Turner","Bent Vad Odgaard","Catharina S. Nisbeth","Heather Moorhouse","Jacob Kidmose","Jesper Olsen","Jolanta Kazmierczak","Kaarina Weckström","Lærke Thorling","Marie-Louise Siggaard-Andersen","Ole Bennike","Suzanne McGowan","Søren Jessen"],"corresponding_author":["Bennike, Ole"],"country":["Finland","Greenland","Norway","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Geological Survey of Denmark and Greenland","Lancaster University","Smithsonian Institution","UiT The Arctic University of Norway","University of Helsinki","University of Nottingham"],"keyword":["Holocene","Inorganic phosphorus","Macrofossils","Pigments","Soil erosion"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-KUM"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-KUM","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"early historical forest clearance caused major degradation of water quality at lake væng denmark","src":["au","ku","kum"],"src-code":["DI-GV-KUM","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/321196033/early_historical_forest.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S03-092.63-015229","identifier":[{"id":"85111486771","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000696950600004","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-241276b0-0634-4dd4-b031-7362650b4091","ku-c47c5236-9617-4289-85d2-06efb406092a","kum-ee1a791c-b6fa-4a43-9af4-343278ca93f7"],"keyword":["Catchment-lake processes","Catchment-lake processes, Macrofossils, Pigments, Inorganic phosphorus, Soil erosion, Holocene","Holocene","Inorganic phosphorus","Macrofossils","Pigments","Soil erosion"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-41208BA310181A2EA59975A614807BA9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/321196033/early_historical_forest.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"kum ministry of culture archives museums and royal library denmark","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-KUM","l3nn":"KUM Ministry of Culture - Archives, Museums, and Royal Library Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KUM Ministry of Culture - Archives, Museums, and Royal Library Denmark","namevar":["Ministry of Culture Denmark, The National Museum of Denmark","Ministry of Culture Denmark, The National Museum of Denmark, Environmental Archaeology and Materials Science"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-KUM","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gl","key":"geological survey of denmark and greenland","loc":["Greenland","Nordic"],"name":"Geological Survey of Denmark and Greenland","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Geoscience"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"gb","key":"university of nottingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Nottingham","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"gb","key":"lancaster university","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Lancaster University","used":1},{"affno":"6","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U"