{"abstract":"Background: Glucocorticoids are among the most commonly prescribed drugs, but there is no biomarker that can quantify their action. The aim of the study was to identify and validate circulating biomarkers of glucocorticoid action. Methods: In a randomized, crossover, single-blind, discovery study, 10 subjects with primary adrenal insufficiency (and no other endocrinopathies) were admitted at the in-patient clinic and studied during physiological glucocorticoid exposure and withdrawal. A randomization plan before the first intervention was used. Besides mild physical and/or mental fatigue and salt craving, no serious adverse events were observed. The transcriptome in peripheral blood mononuclear cells and adipose tissue, plasma miRNAomic, and serum metabolomics were compared between the interventions using integrated multi-omic analysis. Results: We identified a transcriptomic profile derived from two tissues and a multi-omic cluster, both predictive of glucocorticoid exposure. A microRNA (miR-122-5p) that was correlated with genes and metabolites regulated by glucocorticoid exposure was identified (p=0.009) and replicated in independent studies with varying glucocorticoid exposure (0.01 ≤ p≤0.05). Conclusions: We have generated results that construct the basis for successful discovery of biomarker(s) to measure effects of glucocorticoids, allowing strategies to individualize and optimize glucocorticoid therapy, and shedding light on disease etiology related to unphysiological glucocorticoid exposure, such as in cardiovascular disease and obesity. Funding: The Swedish Research Council (Grant 2015-02561 and 2019-01112); The Swedish federal government under the LUA/ALF agreement (Grant ALFGBG-719531); The Swedish Endocrinology Association; The Gothenburg Medical Society; Wellcome Trust; The Medical Research Council, UK; The Chief Scientist Office, UK; The Eva Madura's Foundation; The Research Foundation of Copenhagen University Hospital; and The Danish Rheumatism Association. Clinical trial number: NCT02152553.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Chantzichristos, Dimitrios","Svensson, Per Arne","Garner, Terence","Glad, Camilla Am","Walker, Brian R.","Bergthorsdottir, Ragnhildur","Ragnarsson, Oskar","Trimpou, Penelope","Stimson, Roland H.","Borresen, Stina W.","Feldt-Rasmussen, Ulla","Jansson, Per Anders","Skrtic, Stanko","Stevens, Adam","Johannsson, Gudmundur"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-440280"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biomarkers","Computational Biology","Gene expression","Glucocorticoids","Human","Medicine","Metabolites","Multi omics","Systems biology","microRNA"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"identification of human glucocorticoid response markers using integrated multi omic analysis from a randomized crossover trial","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.62236","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-440280","identifier":[{"id":"33821793","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8024021","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85103995127","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-4e8557fd-7a5f-4867-b880-a493891b12a0"],"keyword":["biomarkers","computational biology","gene expression","glucocorticoids","human","medicine","metabolites","microRNA","multi-omics","systems biology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7EED9518D93B82A83B3E8ABF4D7B6589"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.62236","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/260300604/elife_62236_v1.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Internal Medicine: Endocrinology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Dimitrios","key":"chantzichristospau","last":"Chantzichristos","name":"Chantzichristos, Dimitrios","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Per Arne","key":"svenssonpau","last":"Svensson","name":"Svensson, Per Arne","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Terence","key":"garnerpau","last":"Garner","name":"Garner, Terence","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Camilla Am","key":"gladpau","last":"Glad","name":"Glad, Camilla Am","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Brian R.","key":"walkerpau","last":"Walker","name":"Walker, Brian R.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ragnhildur","key":"bergthorsdottirpau","last":"Bergthorsdottir","name":"Bergthorsdottir, Ragnhildur","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Oskar","key":"ragnarssonpau","last":"Ragnarsson","name":"Ragnarsson, Oskar","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Penelope","key":"trimpoupau","last":"Trimpou","name":"Trimpou, Penelope","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Roland H.","key":"stimsonpau","last":"Stimson","name":"Stimson, Roland H.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Stina W.","key":"borresenpau","last":"Borresen","name":"Borresen, Stina W.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ulla","key":"feldtrasmussenpau","last":"Feldt-Rasmussen","locid":"b5898535-d448-4e2f-9f41-d5199873676b","name":"Feldt-Rasmussen, Ulla","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Per Anders","key":"janssonpau","last":"Jansson","name":"Jansson, Per Anders","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Stanko","key":"skrticpau","last":"Skrtic","name":"Skrtic, Stanko","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Adam","key":"stevenspau","last":"Stevens","name":"Stevens, Adam","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Gudmundur","key":"johannssonpau","last":"Johannsson","name":"Johannsson, Gudmundur","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33821793"],"pub":{"doi":"10.7554/elife.62236","issn":["2050084X"],"journal":"eLife","status":"p","title":"eLife","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85103995127"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4e8557fd-7a5f-4867-b880-a493891b12a0"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Identification of human glucocorticoid response markers using integrated multi-omic analysis from a randomized crossover trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-440280","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Objective: The time until treatment is a crucial factor for the outcome after medical emergencies. One way of reducing transportation time is the use of helicopter-based emergency medical services, but poor weather conditions, commitment to another mission, or technical problems may limit the availability. In these cases, military search and rescue (SAR) helicopters can be used. The aim of this study was to investigate the role of SAR helicopters in the civilian emergency medical system. Methods: We conducted a retrospective study of SAR mission reports and SAR medical records collected from the archives of the Royal Danish Air Force for the years 2016 and 2017. Results: A total of 1,262 mission reports were included with a total of 628 medical records available in the study period. There were 490 of 1,246 (39.3%) missions involving patients. Interhospital transfers accounted for 306 of 604 (50.7%) of cases, whereas island evacuation constituted 110 of 604 (18.2%) cases. The most prevalent suspected diagnosis was acute coronary syndrome (14.4%). The 30-day survival rate was 87.6%. Conclusion: The military SAR helicopters were most commonly activated for civilian emergency medical service mission assistance due to poor weather conditions. The most frequent medical condition was acute coronary syndrome.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Christensen, Rasmus Ejlersgaard","Ottosen, Camilla Ikast","Sonne, Asger","Noernberg, Bo","Juul, Anders","Steinmetz, Jacob","Rasmussen, Lars S"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-447470"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Danish Defence Medical Command"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"search and rescue helicopters for emergency medical service assistance a retrospective study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-447470","identifier":[{"id":"34172236","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85103926240","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-5a5a3afb-714d-4dd7-b847-bb3353fa1182"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AA945A7923698D6BC4E662F8DD22283E"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Danish Defence Medical Command"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Rasmus Ejlersgaard","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Rasmus Ejlersgaard","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Camilla Ikast","key":"ottosenpau","last":"Ottosen","name":"Ottosen, Camilla Ikast","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Asger","key":"sonnepau","last":"Sonne","locid":"e7646f86-edfb-433b-85fe-558015165194","name":"Son