an Heart Journal: Acute Cardiovascular Care","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85164552077"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","North Denmark Region","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-c8c51d87-9155-495d-b310-feee05dd3dc8","au-f8e5724e-df58-4fb1-bd34-c48c02e8740c","ku-5dee65ba-5231-4b81-9b75-56f824f7f6a0"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Haemodynamics and vasopressor support during prolonged targeted temperature management for 48 hours after out-of-hospital cardiac arrest : a post hoc substudy of a randomised clinical trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.00-009638","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVES: To investigate rebound hyperthermia following targeted temperature management after cardiac arrest and its impact on functional outcome. DESIGN: Post hoc analysis. SETTING: Ten European ICUs. PATIENTS: Patients included in the time-differentiated therapeutic hypothermia in out-of-hospital cardiac arrest survivors trial treated with targeted temperature management at 33°C for 48 or 24 hours. Favorable functional outcome was defined as a Cerebral Performance Category of 1 or 2 at 6 months. INTERVENTIONS: None. MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS: Of 338 included patients, 103 (30%) experienced rebound hyperthermia defined as a maximum temperature after targeted temperature management and rewarming exceeding 38.5°C. Using multivariate logistic regression analysis, increasing age (odds ratio, 0.97; 95% CI, 0.95-0.99; p = 0.02) and severe acute kidney injury within 72 hours of ICU admission (odds ratio, 0.35; 95% CI, 0.13-0.91; p = 0.03) were associated with less rebound hyperthermia, whereas male gender (odds ratio, 3.94; 95% CI, 1.34-11.57; p = 0.01), highest C-reactive protein value (odds ratio, 1.04; 95% CI, 1.01-1.07; p = 0.02), and use of mechanical chest compression during cardiopulmonary resuscitation (odds ratio, 2.00; 95% CI, 1.10-3.67; p = 0.02) were associated with more rebound hyperthermia. Patients with favorable functional outcome spent less time after rewarming over 38.5°C (2.5% vs 6.3%; p = 0.03), 39°C (0.14% vs 2.7%; p < 0.01), and 39.5°C (0.03% vs 0.71%; p < 0.01) when compared with others. Median time to rebound hyperthermia was longer in the unfavorable functional outcome group (33.2 hr; interquartile range, 14.3-53.0 hr vs 6.5 hr; interquartile range, 2.2-34.1; p < 0.01). In a predefined multivariate binary logistic regression model, rebound hyperthermia was associated with decreased odds of favorable functional outcome (odds ratio, 0.42; 95% CI, 0.22-0.79). CONCLUSIONS: One-third of targeted temperature management patients experience rebound hyperthermia, and it is more common in younger male patients with an aggravated inflammatory response and those treated with a mechanical chest compression device. Later onset of rebound hyperthermia and temperatures exceeding 38.5°C associate with unfavorable outcome.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Holm, Aki","Kirkegaard, Hans","Taccone, Fabio Silvio","Søreide, Eldar","Grejs, Anders M","Toome, Valdo","Hassager, Christian","Rasmussen, Bodil Steen","Laitio, Timo","Storm, Christian","Hästbacka, Johanna","Skrifvars, Markus B"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-009228"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Belgium","Estonia","Finland","Germany","Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin","Helsinki University Hospital","Hôpital Erasme","North Estonia Medical Centre","North Estonia Medical Centre Foundation","Stavanger University Hospital","Turku University Central Hospital","University Hospitals Brussels (UZB)","University of Bergen","University of Helsinki","University of Turku","Université libre de Bruxelles"],"keyword":["Fever","Out of hospital cardiac arrest","Resuscitation","Targeted temperature management"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"factors associated with rebound hyperthermia after targeted temperature management in out of hospital cardiac arrest patients an explorative substudy of the time differentiated therapeutic hypothermia in out of hospital cardiac arrest survivors trial","src":["aau","au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft