{"abstract":"Tumor-associated macrophages are among the most abundant non-cancerous cells in the tumor microenvironment and in many cancers macrophage infiltration into the tumor is associated with poor prognosis. Macrophages contribute to tumor development by promoting angiogenesis and immune suppression, and display remarkable phenotypic heterogeneity in the tumor microenvironment. Therapeutic strategies targeting macrophages that currently are in clinical development are mainly focused on a general depletion of tumor-associated macrophages, either by targeting the CSF-1/CSF-1R axis or by inhibiting macrophage recruitment by blocking CCR2/CCL2 signaling. Despite good pre-clinical response rates the treatment strategies focusing on general macrophage targeting have only shown limited clinical success and new approaches that target specific subsets of tumo-associated macrophages are emerging. This chapter will briefly present the functions and heterogeneity of tumor-associated macrophages and provide an overview of the current state of clinical development for pan-targeting strategies as well as discuss new strategies for targeting specific macrophage subsets for future anti-tumor immunotherapies.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Rasmussen, Rikke Kongsgaard","Etzerodt, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-224295"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["CCR2","CD163","CD206","CLEVER 1","Heterogeneity","TREM2","Tumor associated macrophages"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"therapeutic targeting of tumor associated macrophages","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85107828244","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-224295","identifier":[{"id":"34099108","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85107828244","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-a6a0e90d-e3cf-4117-b9a2-772bfd63ef39"],"keyword":["CCR2","CD163","CD206","CLEVER-1","CSFR-1","Heterogeneity","MARCO","TREM2","Tumor-associated macrophages"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8147877E9A7A6B2BA239C9DE6B7959AB"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Biomedicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke Kongsgaard","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"dab4a423-a220-40f1-b4f9-4100e8ac815a","name":"Rasmussen, Rikke Kongsgaard","orcid":"0000-0002-3366-7931","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"etzerodtpau","last":"Etzerodt","locid":"c0b60588-6275-45c0-8ad2-bb91a248ff22","name":"Etzerodt, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-6757-2068","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34099108"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/bs.apha.2021.03.002","isbn":["9780128235621"],"issn":["10543589"],"pages":"185-211","place":"Cambridge","publisher":"Academic Press","series":"Advances in Pharmacology","status":"p","title":"Advances in Immunopharmacology"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85107828244"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-a6a0e90d-e3cf-4117-b9a2-772bfd63ef39"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Therapeutic targeting of tumor-associated macrophages","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-224295","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Programmed cell death receptor ligand-1 (PD-L1) expression, KRAS (KRASm) and EGFR (EGFRm) mutations may influence non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) prognosis. We aimed to evaluate PD-L1 expression, KRASm, and EGFRm and survival among stage III unresected NSCLC patients. Using Danish registries, we collected data on stage III unresected NSCLC patients diagnosed 2001–2012 and paraffin-embedded tumor tissue from pathology archives. We assessed PD-L1 expression in tumors and tumor-infiltrating immune cells (ICs) by immunohistochemistry (≥ 1% threshold for PD-L1+). We genotyped KRAS and EGFR. Follow-up extended from 120 days post-diagnosis to death, emigration, or 31/12/2014. We computed median survival using Kaplan–Meier methods, and hazard ratios (HRs) using Cox regression associating the biomarkers with death, adjusting for confounders. Among 305 patients, 48% had adenocarcinoma; 38% squamous cell carcinoma. Forty-nine percent had PD-L1+ tumors—51% stage IIIA and 26% KRASm. Few (2%) patients had EGFRm. Median survival in months was 14.7 (95% CI = 11.8–17.9) and 13.4 (95% CI = 9.5–16.3) in PD-L1+ and PD-L1− tumors, respectively. KRASm was not associated with death (HR = 1.06, 95% CI = 0.74–1.51 versus wildtype). PD-L1+ tumors yielded a HR = 0.83 (95% CI = 0.63–1.10); PD-L1+ ICs a HR = 0.51 (95% CI = 0.39–0.68). Tumor expression of PD-L1 did not influence survival. PD-L1+ ICs may confer survival benefit in stage III unresected NSCLC patients.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Cronin-Fenton, Deirdre","Dalvi, Tapashi","Movva, Naimisha","Pedersen, Lars","Hansen, Hanh","Fryzek, Jon","Hedgeman, Elizabeth","Mellemgaard, Anders","Rasmussen, Torben Riis","Shire, Norah","Hamilton-Dutoit, Stephen Jacques","Nørgaard, Mette"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-274542"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Cronin-Fenton, Deirdre"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","AstraZeneca UK"],"keyword":["Aged","B7 H1 Antigen metabolism","Carcinoma Non Small Cell Lung genetics","Cohort Studies","Confidence Intervals","Denmark","ErbB Receptors metabolism","Female","Humans","Kaplan Meier Estimate","Lung Neoplasms genetics","Male","Mutation genetics","Neoplasm Staging","Proto Oncogene Proteins p21 ras genetics","Survival Analysis"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"pd l000000000001 expression egfr and kras mutations and survival among stage iii unresected non small cell lung cancer patients a danish cohort study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-96486-2","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-274542","identifier":[{"id":"85113254032","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34413420","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC8377072","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-fe7640cc-63a5-4705-af25-721e1b971d0e"],"keyword":["Aged","B7-H1 Antigen/metabolism","Carcinoma, Non-Small-Cell Lung/genetics","Cohort Studies","Confidence Intervals","Denmark","ErbB Receptors/metabolism","Female","Humans","Kaplan-Meier Estimate","Lung Neoplasms/genetics","Male","Mutation/genetics","Neoplasm Staging","Proto-Oncogene Proteins p21(ras)/genetics","Survival Analysis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8BA8FD95934AFBB97C74F7878CF31185"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-96486-2","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Uni