,"type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.93-049080","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: Identifying biomarkers for abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAA) could prove beneficial in prognosis of AAA and thus the selection for treatment. Microfibrillar-associated protein 4 (MFAP4) is an extracellular matrix protein that is highly expressed in aorta. MFAP4 is involved in several tissue remodeling-related diseases. We aimed to investigate the potential role of plasma MFAP4 (pMFAP4) as a biomarker of AAA. METHODS: Plasma samples and data were obtained for 504 male AAA patients and 188 controls in the Viborg Vascular (VIVA) screening trial. The pMFAP4 levels were measured by Alphalisa. The Mann-Whitney U test assessed differences in pMFAP4 levels between the presence and absence of different exposures of interest. The correlation between pMFAP4 and aorta growth rate were investigated through spearman's correlation analysis. Immunohistochemistry and multiple logistic regression adjusted for potential confounders assessed the association between pMFAP4 and AAA. Multiple linear regression assessed the correlation between pMFAP4 and aorta growth rate. Cox regression and competing risk regression were used to investigate the correlation between AAA patients with upper tertile pMFAP4 and the risk of undergoing later surgical repair. RESULTS: A significant negative correlation between pMFAP4 and aorta growth rate was observed using spearman's correlation analysis (ρ = -0.14; P = .0074). However, this finding did not reach significance when applying multiple linear regression. A tendency of decreased pMFAP4 was observed in AAA using immunohistochemistry. Competing risk regression adjusted for potential confounders indicated that patients with upper tertile pMFAP4 had a hazard ratio of 0.51 (P = .001) for risk of undergoing later surgical repair. CONCLUSIONS: High levels of pMFAP4 are associated with a decreased likelihood of receiving surgical repair in AAA. This observation warrants confirmation in an independent cohort.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Lindholt, Jes S.","Madsen, Mathilde","Kirketerp-Møller, Katrine Lindequist","Schlosser, Anders","Kristensen, Katrine Lawaetz","Behr-Rasmussen, Carsten","Sorensen, Grith Lykke"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-021363"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Sorensen, Grith Lykke"],"keyword":["Aged","Aortic Aneurysm Abdominal blood","Biomarkers blood","Carrier Proteins blood","Denmark","Disease Progression","Extracellular Matrix Proteins blood","Extracellular matrix","Glycoproteins blood","Growth rate","Humans","MFAP4","Male","Predictive Value of Tests","Prospective Studies","Risk Assessment","Risk Factors","Surgical repair","Time Factors","Up Regulation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"high plasma microfibrillar associated protein 000000000004 is associated with reduced surgical repair in abdominal aortic aneurysms","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jvs.2019.08.253","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-021363","identifier":[{"id":"31784280","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85076226066","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-76fb0e39-2336-4a95-9926-ca979352994c","sdu-6fbd7832-5fb5-4241-b2d1-87d6e21a0a79"],"keyword":["Abdominal aorta aneurysms","Aged","Aortic Aneurysm, Abdominal/blood","Biomarkers/blood","Carrier Proteins/blood","Denmark","Disease Progression","Extracellular Matrix Proteins/blood","Extracellular matrix","Glycoproteins/blood","Growth rate","Humans","MFAP4","Male","Predictive Value of Tests","Prospective Studies","Risk Assessment","Risk Factors","Surgical repair","Time Factors","Up-Regulation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-4E08C93067A297A715786983F9848007"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/159859233/PIIS0741521419323146.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/220252845/1_s2.0_S0741521419323146_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jvs.2019.08.253","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Karkirurgi, Viborg"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Regionshospitalet Viborg"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Molecular Medicine, Department of Cancer and Inflammation Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jes Sanddal","key":"lindholtpau","last":"Lindholt","locid":"8c04d12e-9