ngs of Ixodus ricinus ticks from a tick-borne encephalitis virus micro-focus in Northern Zealand, Denmark","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-092.28-038087","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Objectives: Little is known about the distribution and intergenerational transmission of attachment representations in ADHD populations and further how attachment may influence the prognosis of ADHD. In this study we aimed to investigate attachment distribution, intergenerational transmission and the potential impact of maternal and child attachment representations on treatment response in a sample of children with ADHD. Methods: Sixty mother-child dyads from families with offspring ADHD were recruited. Attachment representations were assessed with the Adult Attachment Interview (AAI) and the Child Attachment Interview (CAI). ADHD symptom severity was assessed at four time points with the ADHD-Rating Scale. Results: Of the children, only 15% and among the mothers, only 23% were securely attached. Contrary to predictions, the association between maternal and child attachment representations (Secure versus Insecure) did not reach statistical significance as we found overall concordance rate to be 63% (kappa = −0.05). Neither child nor maternal attachment significantly predicted treatment response. Conclusions: In families with offspring ADHD, the prevalence of insecure attachment was remarkably high in the children as well as in the mothers. Intergenerational transmission of attachment was very low and in short-term follow up, attachment representations did not affect treatment outcome. Our findings support previous research in suggesting that the relationship between ADHD and Insecure attachment is complex. Nevertheless, the importance of considering attachment as a factor in the treatment, functional impairment and long term prognosis of children with ADHD remains.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Darling Rasmussen, Pernille","Bilenberg, Niels","Shmueli-Goetz, Yael","Simonsen, Erik","Bojesen, Anders Bo","Storebø, Ole Jakob"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-042060"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Rasmussen, Pernille Darling"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Anna Freud National Centre for Children and Families"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"attachment representations in mothers and their children diagnosed with adhd distribution transmission and impact on treatment outcome","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85061202981","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-042060","identifier":[{"id":"85061202981","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-47ce4c41-0f46-4b51-83ed-f7100a9d35af","sdu-db9f38d6-a722-4ebc-931f-b4100ce766b9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-D395B204BFA1B69B3FA51702D3648121"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, Research Unit of Child and Adolescent Psychiatry (Southern Denmark)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Psykiatrien Region Sjælland"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"anna freud national centre for children and families","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Anna Freud National Centre for Children and Families","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Psychiatry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Pernille","key":"darlingrasmussenpau","last":"Darling Rasmussen","locid":"a3bed430-277a-4f8d-9e44-38f7cb7e65f8","name":"Rasmussen, Pernille Darling","name_org":"Darling Rasmussen, Pernille","orcid":"0000-0002-4649-5745","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Niels","key":"bilenbergpau","last":"Bilenberg","locid":"c865af0f-affd-44a4-b9fa-39c26141d3e2","name":"Bilenberg, Niels","orcid":"0000-0002-5838-556X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Yael","key":"shmueligoetzpau","last":"Shmueli-Goetz","name":"Shmueli-Goetz, Yael","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Erik","key":"simonsenpau","last":"Simonsen","locid":"a00e5cad-92bc-40ab-8ebc-92298c919f9d","name":"Simonsen, Erik","orcid":"0000-0002-6134-2392","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Bo","key":"bojesenpau","last":"Bojesen","locid":"c4eb0319-8212-4e4b-a396-e5cb4f018bae","name":"Bojesen, Anders Bo","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ole Jakob","key":"storebøpau","last":"Storebø","locid":"fc9ed7a4-77cd-49cf-8a3f-679597f41d6f","name":"Storebø, Ole Jakob","orcid":"0000-0003-0204-7745","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s10826-019-01344-5","issn":["10621024","15732843"],"issn_org":["10621024"],"issue":"4","journal":"Journal of Child and Family Studies","pages":"1018-1028","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Journal of Child and Family Studies","volume":"28"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85061202981"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-47ce4c41-0f46-4b51-83ed-f7100a9d35af","sdu-db9f38d6-a722-4ebc-931f-b4100ce766b9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Attachment Representations in Mothers and Their Children Diagnosed with ADHD : Distribution, Transmission and Impact on Treatment Outcome","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-042060","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background and purpose — Using contemporary indications, up to 50% of patients undergoing knee arthroplasty are eligible for unicompartmental knee arthroplasty (UKA), and lower UKA use likely reflects a restrictive approach to patient selection. Since broader indications have been successfully introduced, and low surgical volume and UKA percentage (usage) are associated with higher revision rates, it is of interest whether the actual use of UKA has changed accordingly. We explored this by assessing time trends in patient demographics and whether these are associated with center UKA volume and usage. Patients and methods — From the Danish Knee Arthroplasty Registry, we included 8,501 medial UKAs performed for primary osteoarthritis during 2002–2016. Using locally weighted regression, we examined changes—both overall and by center volume and usage (low vs high)—in sex distribution, age, weight, and preoperative American Knee Society Score (AKSS-O). Results — Over the last 20 years, UKA use in Denmark has been increasing steadily. Age, weight, and proportion of men all increased regardless of volume and usage. AKSS-O showed an initial increase followed by a decrease. In low-usage and low-volume centers, the proportion of women was higher, patients were younger, weighed less, and had higher AKSS-O scores; however, for age and AKSS-O, the groups were converging during the last part of the period. Interpretation — Characteristics of UKA patients have changed in the last 15 years irrespective of center volume and usage. We found between-group differences for both volume and usage, though with convergence for age and AKSS-O, which suggests an increasingly uniform approach to patient selection.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Henkel, Cecilie","Mikkelsen, Mette","Pedersen, Alma Becic","Rasmussen, Lasse E.","Gromov, Kirill","Price, Andrew","Troelsen, Anders"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-025463"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Henkel, Cecilie"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["England","NATIONAL JOINT REGISTRY","Outcomes","Revision","Surgeon volume"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"medial unicompartmental knee arthroplasty increasingly uniform patient demographics despite differences in surgical volume and usage a descriptive study of 000000000008 000000000501 cases from the danish knee arthroplasty registry","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17453674.2019.1601834","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-100.00-025463","identifier":[{"id":"85064158320","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000466301500001","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC6718175","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"30973052","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-afb5a001-941f-4024-8b4d-40bccd9dfe02","ku-5a6e5b4a-c48d-4493-8cc5-b9a81a3a9f9b","sdu-c7e55dfa-b0aa-4f2e-8190-62fe500b89fb"],"keyword":["ENGLAND","MATCHED PATIENTS","NATIONAL JOINT REGISTRY","OUTCOMES","REVISION","SURGEON VOLUME"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-923872079518B2F1227F385FBE5CCF4D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17453674.2019.1601834","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/149502191/Medial_unicompartmental_knee_arthroplasty_increasingly_uniform_patient_d