b","key":"nielsenjacobpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"bfca7bf8-41f7-4f6d-af41-38b9d20dbb8a","name":"Nielsen, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0002-5134-8950","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jørgen","key":"maagaardjørgenpau","last":"Maagaard","locid":"4aa7409b-3c80-423f-bcf1-1fa99bf69b25","name":"Maagaard, Jørgen","orcid":"0000-0003-3723-070X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jakob Lindberg","key":"nielsenjakoblindbergpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"6ccdf2a1-ca2a-4797-a19b-ed6051f7f4b6","name":"Nielsen, Jakob Lindberg","orcid":"0000-0003-0979-4647","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Gitte","key":"rasmussengittepau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"02206f50-a2a4-40ae-aee8-8a2fa9713ebb","name":"Rasmussen, Gitte","orcid":"0000-0002-2940-0839","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dennis","key":"daydennispau","last":"Day","locid":"0773744d-3689-40ba-b9d0-cc69e6d62dec","name":"Day, Dennis","orcid":"0000-0002-9917-7986","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-9516c9b8-39c5-4e61-813c-211f1d54a0f4"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Natural Kinesthetic Interaction and Social Relations Between Training-Robots and Their Users","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-615914","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Academic Spin-Offs (ASOs) are born with the mission to bring to market science and technology innovations. Nevertheless, their relative weak financial performance has been a focus of discussion. Prior research has explored factors that explain part of the performance diversity, including aspects related to the academic entrepreneur (e.g. human capital), organizational (e.g. team characteristics, board composition, network of partners), and institutional factors (e.g. university context, support mechanisms). We argue that the diversity of technologies that these firms are based on is an additional source of performance heterogeneity. Academic spin-offs transition from a research to a market context is complex. The technology novelty in ASOs generates a staged challenge for the academic entrepreneur. First, identifying a market and activating the first sale; second, generating a sustainable revenue stream that brings growth to the ASO. However, we have still a limited knowledge on the relationship between technology innovativeness and firm performance in the ASOs context. This study tests the relationship between innovativeness dimensions, across different industries, and the observed firm (ASOs) performance. A longitudinal sample, between 2000 and 2012, of 125 academic spin-offs of Norway provides valuable insights on how the origin and characteristics of the technology relates to differences in market performance. The results show how different dimensions of innovativeness have a different impact on ASOs’ market performance, offering a nuanced view of the relationship between innovativeness and performance in this context. The findings provide insights for policy makers and university managers interested in understanding the effects of their policy choices on academic spin-offs.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Giones, Ferran","Billström, Anders","Rasmussen, Einar","Novotny, Adam"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-638116"],"event":{"end":"2017-06-09","place":"Padova, it","start":"2017-06-08","title":"Moving Knowledge into Action: A Debate on Academic Entrepreneurship","year":"2017"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Nord University"],"keyword":["Academic entrepreneurship","Entrepreneurship","Innovation","University industry collaboration"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the effects of innovativeness on academic spin offs performance","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-638116","ids":["sdu-e291d709-9b56-41c8-b62b-be1d0810e545"],"keyword":["Academic entrepreneurship","Innovation","University-Industry Collaboration","entrepreneurship"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E7D3912FAD3AB6668DB22390A821034C"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Department of Technology and Innovation, SDU Technology Entrepreneurship and Innovation","SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Mads Clausen Institute (MCI)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"nord university","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Nord University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ferran","key":"gionespau","last":"Giones","locid":"907136be-386d-4797-a898-9e0fabf933ba","name":"Giones, Ferran","orcid":"0000-0002-1927-6918","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"billströmpau","last":"Billström","name":"Billström, Anders","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Einar","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","name":"Rasmussen, Einar","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Adam","key":"novotnypau","last":"Novotny","name":"Novotny, Adam","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-e291d709-9b56-41c8-b62b-be1d0810e545"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The Effects of Innovativeness on Academic Spin-offs Performance","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-638116","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Rationale: Abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA) is a complex disease with both genetic and environmental risk factors. Together, 6 previously identified risk loci only explain a small proportion of the heritability of AAA. Objective: To identify additional AAA risk loci using data from all available genome-wide association studies. Methods and Results: Through a meta-analysis of 6 genome-wide association study data sets and a validation study totaling 10 204 cases and 107 766 controls, we identified 4 new AAA risk loci: 1q32.3 (SMYD2), 13q12.11 (LINC00540), 20q13.12 (near PCIF1/MMP9/ZNF335), and 21q22.2 (ERG). In various database searches, we observed no new associations between the lead AAA single nucleotide polymorphisms and coronary artery disease, blood pressure, lipids, or diabetes mellitus. Network analyses identified ERG, IL6R, and LDLR as modifiers of MMP9, with a direct interaction between ERG and MMP9. Conclusions: The 4 new risk loci for AAA seem to be specific for AAA compared with other cardiovascular diseases and related traits suggesting that traditional cardiovascular risk factor management may only have limited value in preventing the progression of aneurysmal disease.","author_count":118,"contributors":["Jones, Gregory T","Tromp, Gerard","Kuivaniemi, Helena","Gretarsdottir, Solveig","Baas, Annette F","Giusti, Betti","Strauss, Ewa","van 't Hof, Femke N","Webb, Thomas","Erdman, Robert","Ritchie, Marylyn D","Elmore, James R","Verma, Anurag","Pendergrass, Sarah","Kullo, Iftikhar J","Ye, Zi","Peissig, Peggy L","Gottesman, Omri","Verma, Shefali S","Malinowski, Jennifer","Rasmussen-Torvik, Laura J","Borthwick, Kenneth","Smelser, Diane T","Crosslin, David R","de Andrade, Mariza","Ryer, Evan J","McCarty, Catherine A","Bottinger, Erwin P","Pacheco, Jennifer A","Crawford, Dana C","Carrell, David S","Gerhard, Glenn S","Franklin, David P","Carey, David J","Phillips, Victoria L","Williams, Michael J","Wei, Wenhua","Blair, Ross","Hill, Andrew A","Vasudevan, Thodur M","Lewis, David R","Thomson, Ian A","Krysa, Jo","Hill, Geraldine B","Roake, Justin","Merriman, Tony R","Oszkinis, Grzegorz","Galora, Silvia","Saracini, Claudia","Abbate, Rosanna","Pulli, Raffaele","Pratesi, Carlo","Saratzis, Athanasios","Verissimo, Anna","Bumpstead, Suzannah J","Badger, Stephen A","Clough, Rachel E","Cockerill, Gillian W","Hafez, Hany","Scott, D J","Futers, T S","Romaine, Simon P","Bridge, Katherine","Griffin, Kathryn J","Bailey, Marc A","Smith, Alberto","Thompson, Matt M","van Bockxmeer, Frank","Matthiasson, Stefan E","Thorleifsson, Gudmar","Thorsteinsdottir, Unnur","Blankensteijn, Jan D","Teijink, Joep A","Wijmenga, Cisca","de Graaf, Jacqueline","Kiemeney, Lambertus A","Lindholt, Jes Sanddal","Hughes, Anne E","Bradley, Declan T","Stirrups, Kathleen","Golledge, Jonathan","Norman, Paul E","Powell, Janet T","Humphries, Steve E","Hamby, Stephen E","Goodall, Alison H","Nelson, Christopher P","Sakalihasan, Natzi","Courtois, Audrey","Ferrell, Robert E","Eriksson, Per","Folkersen, Lasse","Franco-Cereceda, Anders","Eicher, John D","Johnson, Andrew D","Betsholtz, Christer","Ruusalepp, Arno","Franzén, Oscar","Schadt, Eric","Björkegren, Johan L","Lipovich, Leonard","Drolet, Anne M","Verhoeven, Eric","Zeebregts, Clark J","Geelkerken, Robert H","van Sambeek, Marc R","van Sterkenburg, Steven M","de Vries, Jean-Paul P","Stefansson, Kari","Thompson, John R","de Bakker, Paul I","Deloukas, Panos","Sayers, Robert D","Harrison, Seamus","van Rij, Andre M","Samani, Nilesh J","Bown, Matthew J"],"cuid":["S02-086.97-036088"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"51-200 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Africa","America, North","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia","Belgium","Estonia","Iceland","Italy","Netherlands","New Zealand","Poland","Saudi Arabia","South Africa","Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations