{"abstract":"Abstract: Background:The risk of ischemic stroke/thromboembolic events after an intracranial haemorrhage (ICH) in atrial fibrillation (AF) patients on warfarin treatment is poorly characterised. The aim of this study was to assess the association between the risk of ischaemic stroke/thromboembolic events and ICH. Methods:Linkage of three Danish nationwide administrative registries in the period between 1999-2012 identified AF patients on warfarin treatment. Event rate ratios of stroke/thromboembolic events, major bleeding and all-cause mortality stratified by ICH were calculated, and Cox proportional hazard models were used to compare event rates among ICH survivors. A matched odds ratio (OR) was calculated for ICH occurrences within 0-3 months relative to 3-6 months prior to a stroke/thromboembolic event. A rate ratio of claimed warfarin prescriptions in a 3-month period pre- and post-ICH was also calculated. Results:We studied 58,815 AF patients (median age 72.6 years; 60% male). When compared to the non-ICH group, the ICH group was at increased risk for stroke/SE/TIA [Rate Ratio 3.67 (95% confidence interval [CI], 3.12 to 4.31] and mortality [5.55 (95% CI, 5.20 to 5.92)], but not for major bleeding [1.06 (95% CI, 0.81 to 1.39)]. The matched OR of ICH occurrences prior to a stroke/SE/TIA was 4.33 (95% CI, 2.44 to 8.15). The rate ratio of claimed warfarin prescriptions post and pre-ICH event was 0.28 (95% CI, 0.24 to 0.33). Conclusion:In this large-scale study of AF patients treated with warfarin, first-time ICH was associated with an increased rate of ischaemic stroke/SE/TIA and mortality compared to the non-ICH group. There was a decrease in the warfarin prescription purchase rate post-ICH period compared to pre-ICH, which may partly explain the excess risk. Background: The risk of ischemic stroke/thromboembolic events after an intracranial haemorrhage (ICH) in atrial fibrillation (AF) patients on warfarin treatment is poorly characterised. The aim of this study was to assess the association between the risk of ischaemic stroke/thromboembolic events and ICH. Methods: Linkage of three Danish nationwide administrative registries in the period between 1999-2012 identified AF patients on warfarin treatment. Event rate ratios of stroke/thromboembolic events, major bleeding and all-cause mortality stratified by ICH were calculated, and Cox proportional hazard models were used to compare event rates among ICH survivors. A matched odds ratio (OR) was calculated for ICH occurrences within 0-3 months relative to 3-6 months prior to a stroke/thromboembolic event. A rate ratio of claimed warfarin prescriptions in a 3-month period pre- and post-ICH was also calculated. Results: We studied 58,815 AF patients (median age 72.6 years; 60% male). When compared to the non-ICH group, the ICH group was at increased risk for stroke/SE/TIA [Rate Ratio 3.67 (95% confidence interval [CI], 3.12 to 4.31] and mortality [5.55 (95% CI, 5.20 to 5.92)], but not for major bleeding [1.06 (95% CI, 0.81 to 1.39)]. The matched OR of ICH occurrences prior to a stroke/SE/TIA was 4.33 (95% CI, 2.44 to 8.15). The rate ratio of claimed warfarin prescriptions post and pre-ICH event was 0.28 (95% CI, 0.24 to 0.33). Conclusion: In this large-scale study of AF patients treated with warfarin, first-time ICH was associated with an increased rate of ischaemic stroke/SE/TIA and mortality compared to the non-ICH group. There was a decrease in the warfarin prescription purchase rate post-ICH period compared to pre-ICH, which may partly explain the excess risk.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Nielsen, Peter Brønnum","Larsen, Torben Bjerregaard","Gorst-Rasmussen, Anders","Skjøth, Flemming","Rasmussen, Lars Hvilsted","Lip, Gregory Y H"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-064813"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","City Hospital in Birmingham","University of Birmingham"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"intracranial hemorrhage and subsequent ischemic stroke in patients with atrial fibrillation a nationwide cohort study","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://journal.publications.chestnet.org/article.aspx?articleid=1939324","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-064813","identifier":[{"id":"84930896404","type":"scopus"},{"id":"25412369","type":"pmid"},{"id":"WOS:000355837900047","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-32f1dc75-f25a-412e-9b6a-43f164987eff"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-87864A38C72A41FE974E752048F9AD7F"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Aalborg AF Study Group","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Hjertemedicin (Kardiologi) [Klinik Hjerte-Lunge]","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Klinik Hjerte-Lunge","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine, Aalborg Thrombosis Research Unit"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of birmingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Birmingham","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"gb","key":"city hospital in birmingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"City Hospital in Birmingham","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter Brønnum","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"3ce5c24a-cc41-4e4c-a071-9a41f036faf8","name":"Nielsen, Peter Brønnum","orcid":"0000-0003-2922-6970","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Torben Bjerregaard","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"a3c6747f-b9db-488b-8eb9-887d38742880","name":"Larsen, Torben Bjerregaard","orcid":"0000-0002-8485-8674","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"gorstrasmussenpau","last":"Gorst-Rasmussen","locid":"2aa85786-c0b1-42ec-93d4-eb7a75696409","name":"Gorst-Rasmussen, Anders","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Flemming","key":"skjøthpau","last":"Skjøth","locid":"6166e832-f5f5-47be-82bb-07962be7c943","name":"Skjøth, Flemming","orcid":"0000-0003-2975-647X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars Hvilsted","key":"rasmussenpau","last":"Rasmussen","locid":"20cfd847-7f3e-4c77-88c0-32d3432496ed","name":"Rasmussen, Lars Hvilsted","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Gregory Y H","key":"lippau","last":"Lip","locid":"8fa30e84-7381-41b4-8037-afc032238a3d","name":"Lip, Gregory Y H","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25412369"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1378/chest.14-2099","issn":["00123692","19313543"],"issn_org":["00123692"],"issue":"6","journal":"Chest","pages":"1651-1658","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Chest","volume":"147"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84930896404"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-32f1dc75-f25a-412e-9b6a-43f164987eff"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Intracranial hemorrhage and subsequent ischemic stroke in patients with atrial fibrillation : A nationwide cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-064813","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: Current diagnostic tests for diffuse large B-cell lymphoma use the updated WHO criteria based on biologic, morphologic, and clinical heterogeneity. We propose a refined classification system based on subset-specific B-cell-associated gene signatures (BAGS) in the normal B-cell hierarchy, hypothesizing that it can provide new biologic insight and diagnostic and prognostic value. PATIENTS AND METHODS: We combined fluorescence-activated cell sorting, gene expression profiling, and statistical modeling to generate BAGS for naive, centrocyte, centroblast, memory, and plasmablast B cells from normal human tonsils. The impact of BAGS-assigned subtyping was analyzed using five clinical cohorts (treated with cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, and prednisone [CHOP], n = 270; treated with rituximab plus CHOP [R-CHOP], n = 869) gathered across geographic regions, time eras, and sampling methods. The analysis estimated subtype frequencies and drug-specific resistance and included a prognostic meta-analysis of patients treated with first-line R-CHOP therapy. RESULTS: Similar BAGS subtype frequencies were assigned across 1,139 samples from five different cohorts. Among R-CHOP-treated patients, BAGS assignment was significantly associated with overall survival and progression-free survival within the germinal center B-cell-like subclass; the centrocyte subtype had a superior prognosis compared with the centroblast subtype. In agreement with the observed therapeutic outcome, centrocyte subtypes were estimated as being less resistant than the centroblast subtype to doxorubicin and vincristine. The centroblast subtype had a complex genotype, whereas the centrocyte subtype had high TP53 mutation and insertion/deletion frequencies and expressed LMO2, CD58, and stromal-1-signature and major histocompatibility complex class II-signature genes, which are known to have a positive impact on prognosis. CONCLUSION: Further development of a diagnostic platform using BAGS-assigned subtypes may allow pathogenetic studies to improve disease management.","author_count":23,"contributors":["Dybkær, Karen","Bøgsted, Martin","Larsen, Steffen Falgreen","Bødker, Julie Støve","Kjeldsen, Malene Krag","Schmitz, Alexander","Bilgrau, Anders Ellern","Xu-Monette, Zijun Y","Li, Ling","Bergkvist, Kim Steve","Laursen, Maria Bach","Rodrigo-Domingo, Maria","Marques, Sara Correia","Rasmussen, Sophie Bech","Nyegaard, Mette","Gaihede, Michael","Møller, Michael B","Samworth, Richard J","Shah, Rajen D","Johansen, Preben","El-Galaly, Tarec Christoffer","Young, Ken H","Johnsen, Hans Erik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-075166"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"diffuse large b cell lymphoma classification system that associates normal b cell subset phenotypes with prognosis","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2014.57.7080","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-075166","identifier":[{"id":"84927639269","type":"scopus"},{"id":"25800755","type":"pmid"},{"id":"WOS:000356058800012","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-567ab058-2d65-4b0d-bffe-e8f8c5a89a26"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E7480EE4599890C6FEC9278C8779520E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2014.57.7080","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic",